\par }{\fs28\cf6 Ability Scores for Monsters}{\fs24 
\par 
\par \tab Another way to modify standard monsters is to assign ability scores to them. This method is more time consuming than applying a simple modifier to m
ake the monster bigger or stronger, but it allows for greater variety. Generating ability scores for a monster also makes it possible for the creature to undertake actions during an encounter that might require an ability check, and it makes opposed check
s between monsters and characters possible. (How likely is that fighter to win a tug\_o\_war with a giant toad?)
\par \tab The system presented here is intended for use with nonhumanoid creatures (humanoids are best treated as characters, see }{\i\fs24 The Complete Book of Humanoids}{\fs24 
 for extensive examples), but it can be used with any creature in a pinch. Start by noting the Size and Intelligence ratings from the creature\rquote 
s description. These two ratings determine the range of ability scores the creature can have (see Tables 6\endash 11).
\par \tab To generate an ability score, roll 3d6 on the appropriate table and read the result from the applicable column. For example, a rust monster is a man\_
sized creature with animal Intelligence. To generate a Strength score for a rust monster, roll 3d6 on Table 5 and read the result from the M column; a roll of 10 yields a Strength score of 12. To generate the rust monster\rquote 
s Intelligence score, roll 3d6 on Table 8 and read the result from the A column; a roll of 18 yields an Intelligence score of 4\emdash an Einst
ein among rust monsters. The creature gains all the bonuses and penalties associated with its actual ability score as listed in the Player\rquote s Handbook. The notes below contain additional information.
\par \tab When you have finished generating the creature\rquote s ability scores, it might be necessary to recalculate its XP value according to the rules in the }{\i\fs24 Dungeon Master Guide}{\fs24 
, Chapter 8. For example, a creature with high Constitution score probably has greater than average hit points and should have a higher XP value than its less robust cousins.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Strength: }{\fs24 Except as noted, all modifiers apply to creatures in the same way they do for characters. Creatures gain the warrior combat bonuses for high scores.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Damage Adjustment:}{\fs24  The bonus or penalty applies to the creature\rquote s natural attacks. If the creature rolls multiple dice to determine damage, the bonus or penalty applies to the total, not to each die.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Weight Allowance:}{\fs24  Add 35 pounds to a creature\rquote 
s weight allowance if it is large, 70 pounds if it is huge, and 105 pounds if it is gargantuan. If published rules list a carrying capacity for a creature, use either the listed capacity or the modified weight allowance from the creature\rquote 
s Strength score, whichever is higher.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Open Doors:}{\fs24  Size L and larger creatures can use this ability to batter down or smash holes in simple wooden walls.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Bend Bars/Lift Gates:}{\fs24  A creature that can bring its full bulk to bear on an object gains the following bonuses by size
 category: S or M, 0; L, +5%; H, +10%; and G, +20%. Exceptionally sturdy objects built to handle heavy loads or restrain large creatures negate the bonus. For example, a griffon would get the bonus when straining against a normal rope but not when pulling
 against a rope made to anchor a ship.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Dexterity: }{\fs24 All modifiers apply to creatures in the same way they apply to characters, except that a creature\rquote s }{\i\fs24 Reaction Adjustment}{\fs24 
 also increases its movement rate. For example, a griffon with a Dexterity score of 16 has a movement rate of 13, Fl 31.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Constitution:}{\b\fs24  }{\fs24 All modifiers apply to creatures in the same way they do for characters. Monsters gain hit point bonuses for high scores as a warrior.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Intelligence:}{\b\fs24  }{\fs24 Creatures that are normally unable to speak do not gain t
hat ability simply by virtue of a high Intelligence score. Any creature with a score at least three points higher than the normal value for its race can understand one or more languages. For example, a horse with an Intelligence score of 4 might know the 
common tongue and be able to give limited responses in the form of hoof taps, whinnies, and shakes of its head. Other than this limitation, Intelligence functions for creatures in the same way it does for characters.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wisdom:}{\b\fs24  }{\fs24 All modifiers apply to creatures in the same way they do for characters.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Charisma:}{\b\fs24  }{\fs24 Reaction and loyalty adjustments apply only to creatures who are able to communicate with each other in meaningful ways. Otherwise, Charisma functions for creatures in the same way it does for characters.
\par 
\par 
