\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\tx2160\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\adjustright \fs20 {\fs24 
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\adjustright {\fs24 \tab The }{\b\fs24 Armor Class Modifier}{\fs24  is subtracted from the creature\rquote 
s base armor class. This modifier is in addition to any Dexterity bonus.
\par 
\par \tab The }{\b\fs24 Hit Die Modifier}{\fs24  is added to the monster\rquote s hit dice. The creature gains all the benefits of the increased hit dice, including reduced THAC0, better saving throws, and more hit p
oints. If the monster is normally given a fixed hit point total, add five hit points per additional hit die.
\par 
\par \tab The number after the slash is for monsters that have hit points divided between different areas of their bodies, such as hydras and beholders. Multiply each area\rquote s hit points by the number. For example, an elder hydra\rquote 
s heads would have 20 hit points each (8 [hp for each head] x 2.5 [elder multiplier] = 20).
\par 
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Legendary Monsters}{\fs24 
\par \tab Just like high\_level heroes, some monsters have progressed in power t
o the point where they are the most fearsome of their kind. Legendary monsters are not just large or strong specimens, but instead they are a superior strain of their race. The rules that follow are designed for non\_
humanoid monsters and can also be combined with the }{\b\fs24 Ability Scores for Monsters}{\fs24  section. 
\par \tab A legendary monster has the same movement rate, number of attacks, damage per attack, morale rating, and special abilities as its normal counterpart. The creature has increased Hit Dice, an improved Armor 
Class, and some additional powers that the DM assigns. Table 12 details typical Hit Dice and Armor Class variances. When creating a legendary monster, don\rquote t forget to recalculate the creature\rquote s experience point value.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 Rank }{\fs24 is simply a convenient way to categorize and assign powers to legendary monsters. Tables 12\endash 22 use the following terms to identify the various types of legendary monsters.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Lesser Scion:}{\fs24  The creature is slightly more powerful than normal members of its race. It comes from an exceptional 
bloodline, but the line has become diluted over the generations. There can be anywhere from several hundred to several thousand lesser scions of a given race on a world, depending on the races\rquote 
 overall population. Lesser scions have two or three powers from Tables 13\endash 22. When generating ability scores for lesser scions, roll 3d4+4.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Scion:}{\fs24  The monster is superior to normal members of its race. There are only a few hundred to perhaps a thousand scions of a given race on a world. Scions have three or four powers from Tables 13\endash 
22. When generating ability scores for scions, roll 2d6+6.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Elder:}{\fs24  The creature is considerably more powerful than normal members of its race. It comes from an exceptional bloodline, barely diluted by time. There are not more than a few 
hundred Elders of any race on a world. Elders have three to five powers fromTables 13\endash 22. When generating ability scores for Elders, roll 1d10+8.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Great Elder:}{\fs24  The creature is vastly more powerful than normal members of its race. It comes from an exception
al and undiluted bloodline or is an outstanding member of an elder bloodline. There are not more than several dozen great elders of a given race on a world. Great elders have four to six powers from Tables 13\endash 
22. When generating ability scores for elders, roll 1d10+8.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Paragon:}{\fs24  The creature represents the pinnacle of its race\rquote 
s strength and vitality. It is an extraordinary member of an elder bloodline, or perhaps the progenitor of its entire race. There are not more than and a handful of paragons of a given
 race on a world, and a paragon often is a unique creature. Paragons have five to seven powers from Tables 13\endash 22. When generating ability scores for paragons, roll 1d8+10.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Powers
\par }{\fs24 
\par \tab A few extra Hit Dice and an Armor Class bonus are not enough to make a legend. This section contains suggestions for the extraordinary powers that legendary creatures possess; feel free to create more. 
\par \tab Not all powers are the same. Just like a dragon\rquote s fear radius is based on its age, the potency of a legendary monster\rquote s power is based upon its overall rank. For example, the lethality of an elder\rquote 
s breath weapon is noticeably different than that of a lesser scion.
\par 
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24 Table 13:
