\par Magic is important to any AD&D campaign, but it is critical to a high\_level world. Chapter 1 discusses the role of magic in more detail. This chapter contains expansions and clarifications to help DMs keep magic manageable and wondrous.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 Daily Recovery of Spells}{\fs24   Wizards and priests cannot simply stop anywhere and regain spells they have cast or change the spells they have memorized. Memorizing a spell is a difficult task that requires a clear head from a good night
\rquote s sleep and 10 minutes of effort per level of the spell (see the}{\i\fs24  Player\rquote s Handbook}{\fs24 , Chapter 7). Memorizing a spell is an arduous mental task, and it is helpful to consider exactly what a character must do to accomplish it.

\par \tab The basic requirement to memorize spells is a good night\rquote s sleep. The character must awake feeling fresh and rested. The DM must decide if a character is rested well enough to regain spells, but about eight hours spent in reasonable comfort
\emdash one cannot regain spells after a night spent in a saddle\emdash is the minimum.
\par \tab The spellcaster must also have enough peace, quiet, and comfort to allow proper concentration on the character\rquote s studies or devotions. Spellcasters do not necessarily have to be sitting in the lap of luxury to regain spells, but their mi
nds must be free from overt distractions, such as combat raging nearby, exposure to inclement weather, or fatigue. Of course, wizards need plenty of light to read their spellbooks by.
\par \tab Priests don\rquote t use spellbooks but must have all the trappings required f
or solemn prayer and meditation. Such trappings include some token of the deity being petitioned for spells, such as a holy symbol, or perhaps prayer at a site that reflects the deity\rquote s nature\emdash 
petitioning a war deity from an ancient battlefield is going to get the deity\rquote s attention.
\par \tab Characters who do not require sleep (due to a magical item, racial ability, or other special circumstance) can acquire spells only once each day and only after eight hours of restful calm; the spellcaster cannot acquire spells 
immediately after movement, combat, spellcasting, or other distractions.
\par \tab If a character is disturbed while studying or praying for a spell (by combat, injury, loud noise, or other distraction), the caster must begin work on the spell again and any time al
ready spent on the spell is lost. For example, Rozmare is poring over her spell books to memorize a }{\i\fs24 fly }{\fs24 
spell. She is seated in a forest glade where her party has spent the night. The sun is shining and the forest is fairly quiet, so all Rozmare needs to d
o is study her books for 30 minutes to memorize the spell. Unfortunately, after Rozmare has been studying for 20 minutes, the local pixies decide to play a prank, pitting Rozmare and her companions against an illusory band of goblin acrobats. When the con
fusion finally dies down, Rozmare has to begin studying her }{\i\fs24 fly}{\fs24  spell all over again, requiring another 30 minutes of uninterrupted study.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Limited Study Time:}{\b\fs24  }{\fs24 It is difficult to keep a clear head during a prolonged mental effort. A spellcaster can spend a 
maximum of eight hours a day acquiring spells. After that much effort, the character can no longer concentrate sufficiently to regain any spells, though the character suffers no other disadvantages.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 Additional Comments on Spells and Magical Items}{\fs24   This section contains new and updated information for adjudicating magic use in your campaign. Items marked with a \'86 are from the }{\i\fs24 Tome of Magic}{\fs24 
, spells marked with a \'87 are from }{\i\fs24 The Complete Wizard\rquote s Handbook}{\fs24 , and all other spells and items are from the}{\i\fs24  Player\rquote s Handbook}{\fs24 
. Some of the entries contain optional material, presented in a separate paragraph on a gray background.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 Wizard Spells
\par }{\fs24 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Abi-Dalzim\rquote s Horrid Wilting\'86:}{\i\fs24  }{\fs24 The maximum damage is 16d8.
\par }{\fs24\cf6 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Alacrity\'86:}{\fs24  This spell can be very useful for reducing spell casting times on low\_magic worlds (see page 47). Apply the local multiplier to a spell\rquote s casting time before calculating the }{\i\fs24 alacrity}{\fs24  spell
\rquote s effect.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Antimagic Shell:}{\fs24  This spell temporarily suppresses magic within its area of effect, but it does not destroy or dispel enchantments or kill magical creatures. The spell has no effect on golems, }{\i\fs24 simulacrums, clones,}{\fs24 
 or other constructs which are imbued with magic during their creation process and are thereafter self\_supporting. Most undead creatures are likewise unaffected. Some of these creatures\rquote 
 special abilities may be temporarily nullified, however (see below). Any creature, including a golem or other construct, that is conjured, summoned or from another plane of existence is hedged out of an }{\i\fs24 antimagic shell.}{\fs24  
\par \tab An }{\i\fs24 antimagic shell}{\fs24  suppresses any spell or spell effect brought into or cast into the area of effect. A hasted character, for example, is not hasted while he remains in the area of effect. Permanent sp
ells are not removed, but cannot be used to produce magical effects within the area of effect. For example, a character who has been resurrected is not harmed by an }{\i\fs24 antimagic shell}{\fs24 
, but a character with a permanent tongues spell loses the ability to converse in an unknown language while within the area of effect.
\par \tab An }{\i\fs24 antimagic shell }{\fs24 suppresses special attacks and innate abilities that function over a distance, including breath weapons, gaze attacks, sonic attacks, and psionics, but not touch\_delivered speci
al attacks such as energy draining or the corrosive effects of green slime. A lich, for example, cannot employ spells within an }{\i\fs24 antimagic shell}{\fs24 
 and its ability to cause fear is suppressed, but its paralyzing touch is still effective. Note that holy water is not magical and is fully effective within an }{\i\fs24 antimagic shell}{\fs24 .
\par \tab An }{\i\fs24 antimagic shell}{\fs24  suppresses most potions and their effects; see the note at potions for details.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Astral Spell:}{\fs24  This spell sends a projection of the caster\rquote s body into the Astral Plane. If 
the caster elects to take other characters along, the spell creates projections of them, too. An astral traveler can enter other planes while projecting, but forms a new physical body, identical to the original, to do so.
\par \tab Only magical items are projected along with a traveler\rquote s body, but normal equipment can be rendered temporarily magical by casting }{\i\fs24 Nystul\rquote s magical aura}{\fs24  , }{\i\fs24 continual light}{\fs24 
, and other spells that temporarily imbue objects with magical  properties. See page 51 for a brief discussion of the effects planar travel has on magical items. (The }{\i\fs24 Planescape}{\fs24  boxed set contains more details.)
\par \tab A traveler\rquote s physical body falls into a deathlike trance and requires no food or water while the caster is projecting. The physical forms of projected magical items become inert on the Prime Material Plane. Damage to a traveler\rquote 
s physical body does not affect the projected form, but the character dies immediately if his physical body is killed. Projected equipment vanishes if its physical form is destroyed.
\par \tab Damage inflicted on an astral traveler\rquote s projected form affects the character normally. If a traveler is damaged when returning to his body the damage must be healed normally.
\par \tab If an astral traveler dies, the character must attempt a system shock roll. If the ro
ll fails, the character dies and any items projected along with him dissolve into nothingness. If the roll succeeds, the traveler is drawn back to his original body and wakes up with one hit point. The process is debilitating and the character cannot cast
 or memorize spells. The character can move at half speed and fight and use proficiencies and other skills, but at a \endash 4 penalty to dice rolls. The restrictions and penalties remain until the character regains at least half of his hit points.
\par \tab A successful }{\i\fs24 dispel magic}{\fs24  cast on a traveler\rquote 
s physical body ends the spell, drawing the traveler back to the Prime Material Plane without being otherwise harmed; any companions accompanying the caster are likewise forcibly returned.
\par \tab While traveling through the Astral Plane, a projected form can move by pure thought; a character\rquote s astral movement rate is 30 times his Intelligence/Reason score.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Blink:}{\fs24  Spellcasting is not possible while blinking.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Clairvoyance:}{\fs24  The spellcaster must describe where the sensor this spell creates is to appear. Once created, the sensor cannot be moved.
\par \tab When placing the sensor, the caster must be precise and state the location in terms he knows or are fairly obvious. For example, the caster cannot place the sensor six inches from Ren the wizard\rquote 
s left ear if he has no idea where Ren is at the moment. He can place the sensor in the exact center of Ren\rquote 
s laboratory if he has a reasonable idea where the laboratory is located. A general location for the sensor is permissible if the location is base
d on something known or obvious to the caster. For example, the caster could specify the exact center of the chamber beyond a closed door nearby.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Clairaudience: }{\fs24 The caster must describe where the sensor this spell creates is to appear, see the clairvoyance spell for details.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Color Spray:}{\fs24  The area of effect for this spell is a plane five feet wide at the caster\rquote s hand, 20 feet long, and 20 feet wide at the far end.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Cone of Cold:}{\fs24  The maximum damage from this spell is 10d4+10 points.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Continual Light:}{\fs24  This wizard spell is not reversible, though the priest\rquote s version is.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Delayed Blast Fireball:}{\fs24  This spell inflicts up to 15d6+15 points of damage.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Dispel Magic:}{\fs24  A }{\i\fs24 dispel magic}{\fs24  spell cast directly upon an unattended magical item automatically renders the item inoperable for 1d4 rounds. If }{\i\fs24 dispel magic}{\fs24 
 is cast upon an item that is in the possession of another creature, the item is unaffected by the dispelling attempt if the creature makes a successful saving throw versus spell. If the creature fails its savi
ng throw, the item is rendered inert for 1d4 rounds.
\par \tab Temporary effects from potions can be dispelled, see the section on potions for details.
\par \tab Permanent spells must be individually targeted to be dispelled, requiring a separate }{\i\fs24 dispel magic}{\fs24  for each perma
nent effect. Unlike a magical item, a permanent spell is destroyed, not temporarily rendered nonoperational, by a successful }{\i\fs24 dispel magic}{\fs24 
. A creature or item never gains a saving throw to avoid a dispelling attempt against its permanent effects, but the }{\i\fs24 dispel magic}{\fs24  is not automatically successful either. The caster of the }{\i\fs24 dispel magic}{\fs24 
 must still be of higher level than the caster of the }{\i\fs24 permanency }{\fs24 spell, and he must still make a successful dispelling roll. More detailed information is found at the }{\i\fs24 permanency}{\fs24  spell description.
\par \tab Casting }{\i\fs24 dispel magic}{\fs24  on a creature or object does not radiate an area of effect. Thus, spells such as }{\i\fs24 stoneskin, minor globe of invulnerability}{\fs24 , and }{\i\fs24 barkskin}{\fs24 
 could not be dispelled as the result of trying to negate the magic of a }{\i\fs24 wand of lightning}{\fs24 .
\par \tab Spells and potions whose basic durations are permanent cannot be dispelled. A }{\i\fs24 cure light wounds}{\fs24  spell or}{\i\fs24  potion of extra-healing,}{\fs24  cannot be dispelled after their healing properties have occurred. A }{\i\fs24 
potion of heroism}{\fs24  could be negated while its effects were in operation, however.
\par \tab A successful }{\i\fs24 dispel magic}{\fs24  versus a 10th\_level spell temporarily negates the spell\rquote s effect for 1d4 rounds. It has no effect against a permanent 10th\_level spell cast on a creature.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 ESP:}{\fs24  The caster perceives the subject\rquote s surface thoughts\emdash 
that is, whatever the subject happens to be thinking about at the time. Note that close interrogation might bring buried thoughts to the surface, but wary individuals can fight off the probe and gain a saving throw against the spell. The subject\rquote 
s Wisdom bonus (or penalty) always applies to the saving throw, along with an additional bonus of up to +4, at the DM\rquote s option.
\par 
\par \tab The bonus depends on how closely the subject wants to guard the sought\_after information. In addition, even seem
ingly innocuous questions could reveal information that the target of the spell desires to keep secret. In cases where there is a conflict between the bonuses listed, always grant the higher bonus.
\par \tab Trivial matters merit no bonus. These include questions related to general knowledge (What flag flies over the keep?) and personal questions whose answers are obvious (What color is your hair?).
\par \tab A +1 bonus to the saving throw is warranted when the subject dislikes the interrogator or if the questioner is asking non\_
threatening but potentially embarrassing requests. For example, the subject is being prompted to reveal a minor transgression, such as overcharging a customer, or admit a minor shortcoming, such as fear of a spouse or military commander.
\par \tab A bonus of +
2 is warranted if the interrogator is hostile to the subject or is asking damaging questions. For example, the subject is prompted to reveal indirectly harmful information, such as where personal treasure is hidden, or is asked to betray a trust.
\par \tab A +3 bonus is granted if the interrogator has attacked the subject or is asking seriously damaging questions. For example, the subject is being prompted to reveal a secret vital to his future, such as military plans or trade secrets.
\par \tab A +4 bonus to the save is warranted if the interrogator has killed one of the subject\rquote 
s companions or is asking deeply personal or damaging questions. For example, the subject is being prompted to reveal information vital to himself or to someone important to him, such as the location of a family heirloom, an employer\rquote 
s daily routine, or a carefully guarded password.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Feather Fall:}{\fs24  This spell can be cast in reaction to a fall or a missile attack, provided the caster has not already made an attack or cast a spell in the current round. I
n the case of an attack, the caster must win initiative to complete the spell before the missile arrives; use the normal initiative procedure from the }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Handbook}{\fs24 
. In the case of a fall, the caster can be assumed to automatically cast this spell at the beginning of any fall of 10 feet or more provided he is not prevented from casting spells (silenced, gagged, etc.). 
\par \tab If the caster is falling an extreme distance (in excess of 120 feet/level), the caster can opt to delay the }{\i\fs24 feather fall}{\fs24  spell so that its duration does not expire before the caster lands. When in doubt about the caster\rquote 
s ability to complete the spell before impact, roll initiative. The caster makes a normal roll, adding +1 for the spell\rquote s casting time, and the DM rolls for
 the fall, adding +1 for each 120 feet of free fall. If the caster loses the initiative roll, impact occurs before the spell is completed.
\par \tab This spell does not provide any method by which the spellcaster can determine the length of a fall. Thus, a wizard falling into a lightless pit has no way to determine if the fall is going to be 10 feet or 1,000 feet.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Fly:}{\fs24  It is important to remember that this spell bestows Maneuverability Class B upon the recipient, which limits the user to turns totaling 180 degrees 
or less per round. This might make it difficult for the user to negotiate a twisting corridor at full speed. Once the flying character has completed his allowable turns, he must either finish the round flying in a straight line or stop.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Fear:}{\fs24  If made permanent, a fear spell causes the recipient to continually radiate a fear aura.
\par \tab When cast on an area, a permanent fea
r effect creates a cone as described in the spell description. The caster can orient the cone in any direction, but the direction cannot be changed thereafter. Creatures entering the cone must save vs. spells or flee for one round per level of the caster 
at the time the spell was cast.
\par \tab When cast on an object or creature, the recipient radiates a cone of fear that can be pointed in any direction the recipient desires once per round as though wielding }{\i\fs24 a wand of fear}{\fs24 . Even though this attack requires no casti
ng time or command word, it still counts as an action for that round and has an initiative modifier of +3.
\par 
\par \tab In the permanent version, the caster is granted a limited ability to shape the spell\rquote s parameters to suit his needs. For example, a creature with a permanent fear aura might be granted a gaze attack with a range of 10\endash 
60 feet, a touch, or a continuous globe of fear with a radius of 5 to 20 feet.
\par \tab An object with a fear aura might cause fear when handled, shed continuous fear in a 5\_ to 20\_foot radius, or inspire fear when viewed clearly.
\par \tab Permanent fear on an area might affect creatures passing though a portal or opening up to 60 by 60 feet, a cube of up to 30 feet per side, a sphere with a radius of up to 20 feet, or a hemisphere with a radius of up t
o 25 feet. The shape and dimensions of this spell cannot be changed once set.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Haste:}{\fs24  A creature who has been subjected to two or more }{\i\fs24 haste-}{\fs24 type effects gains the benefit of only the best of the group. A }{\i\fs24 haste }{\fs24 spell never magnifies the effects of magical items, such as }{
\i\fs24 boots of speed}{\fs24  or a }{\i\fs24 potion of speed}{\fs24 .
\par \tab The one year of magical aging inflicted upon the recipients of this spell requires the recipient to make a system shock roll (see }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Handbook}{\fs24 , Chapter 1); failure results in death. This magical agi
ng only occurs during the first round of the spell\rquote s effect, and multiple }{\i\fs24 haste }{\fs24 spells do not cause additional aging unless their effects overlap. For example, casting an additional }{\i\fs24 haste}{\fs24 
 spell one round before an existing }{\i\fs24 haste}{\fs24  spell ends would cause another year of magical aging once the new spell took effect.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Identify: }{\fs24 Characters seeking to purchase magical items might employ this spell to get some idea what they are buying. Remember that the spellcaster must spend the eight hours preceding the casting 
of this spell purifying the items to be identified. Most NPCs do not allow anyone to keep an item for that long; at least not without a substantial advance payment. Dishonest sellers might pocket the advance and disappear, leaving the PCs with a cursed or
 bogus item.
\par \tab The spell also requires the caster to handle the item, and a good way to keep magic under control in a campaign is to require the caster to actually wear or wield the item as it was intended. This requirement means some cursed items are going 
to affect the caster.
\par \tab Be sure to impose the eight\_point temporary Constitution loss the spell inflicts (which provides enemies with an excellent opportunity to attack the weakened spellcaster). Note that there is a limit to the number different magical pr
operties this spell can reveal during a single casting and that the exact number of charges and magical pluses are never revealed.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Invisibility:}{\fs24  If this spell is made permanent, the recipient gains the ability to become invisible and remain so indefinitel
y. Any attack breaks the invisibility, but the recipient can become invisible again during the next round. The return to invisibility has an initiative modifier of +3, and the recipient can take no action other than normal movement during a round in which
 he becomes invisible.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Light:}{\fs24  The wizard\rquote s version of this spell is not reversible.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Limited Wish:}{\fs24  The magical aging inflicted by this spell is a function of the caster\rquote s natural life span. Typical aging is one year for a human, two years for a halfling or half\_
elf, three years for dwarf, four years for a gnome, and five years for an elf.
\par 
\par \tab This spell functions as a }{\i\fs24 wish}{\fs24  spell in most respects, but it cannot produce wealth or magical items. A}{\i\fs24  limited}{\fs24  }{\i\fs24 wish}{\fs24  can mimic the function of most other spells of 7th l
evel or less. If used to alter reality, the changes must be minor. For example, a single creature automatically hits on its next attack, all opponents currently attacking the caster\rquote s party suffer a \endash 
2 attack penalty for the duration of the encounter, a single creature regains 20\endash 50% of lost hit points, or a single creature fails its next saving throw are all possible uses for the spell.
\par \tab Major changes in reality persist for a limited duration, such as a single creature regaining all lost hit points for 24 hours, a hostile creature becoming cooperative for an hour, or an alert sentry falling asleep at his post.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Magic Mirror:}{\fs24  This spell creates an invisible sensor similar to the one created by a }{\i\fs24 clairvoyance}{\fs24  spell; the sensor has the same visual capabilities as a }{\i\fs24 clairvoyance}{\fs24 
 sensor, but the spellcaster can also employ other spells to enhance the effect (see spell description). As with the }{\i\fs24 clairvoyance}{\fs24 
 spell, the user must state where the sensor is to appear; however, the user is free to state the sensors location with respect to the subject without knowing the subject\rquote s exact location. No matter what the spell\rquote s actual duration, the user
\rquote s knowledge of the subject limits how long this spell can be safely used; see the }{\i\fs24 crystal ball }{\fs24 description in the }{\i\fs24 Dungeon Master Guide}{\fs24  for details.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Magic Staff\'86:}{\fs24  Spells stored in the staff are unusable on worlds rated M4 or less (see page 47). A low\_
magic world does not dispel the stored spells, however, and the spells can be used again if the staff is taken to a world with a higher rating before the }{\i\fs24 magic staff}{\fs24  spell\rquote s duration ends.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Otiluke\rquote s Dispelling Screen\'87:}{\fs24  This spell has no effect on permanent spells unless those effects are in operation at the time the individual wal
ks through the screen. For example, a creature made permanently invisible would become visible when walking through the screen and then disappear again on the other side. The screen must still successfully }{\i\fs24 dispel magic}{\fs24 
 against the spell in order to even briefly negate it. Magical items are likewise unaffected by exposure to a dispelling screen. 
\par \tab Since this spell cannot focus its }{\i\fs24 dispel magic}{\fs24  effect, it cannot destroy permanent spells or negate the powers of magical items.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Permanency:}{\fs24  The caster can use this spell to make another spellcaster\rquote s spell permanent. The }{\i\fs24 permanency}{\fs24  must be cast simultaneously with the spell to be made permanent and the }{\i\fs24 permanency }{\fs24 
caster must touch the other caster.
\par \tab A permanent spell cast upon the caster himself or upon a living creature can be dispelled only by a spellcaster of a level greater than the }{\i\fs24 permanency}{\fs24 
 caster at the time he cast the spell. Further, the dispel effect must be targeted solely upon the caster to be effective (see }{\i\fs24 dispel magic}{\fs24  spell description and the note on }{\i\fs24 dispel magic}{\fs24  in this section).
\par \tab The following spells can be made permanent if the caster uses the spell on himself:
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx2880\tx6480\tx7200\adjustright {\i\fs24 comprehend languages\tab protection from evil
\par detect disease\'87\tab protection from hunger
\par \tab and thirst\'87
