\par \tab missiles
\par detect invisibility\tab protection from paralysis\'86
\par detect life\'87\tab read magic
\par detect magic\tab tongues
\par infravision\tab unseen servant
\par past life\'86\tab 
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\adjustright {\i\fs24 
\par }{\fs24 \tab The following spells can be made permanent if cast on a creature other than the }{\i\fs24 permanency}{\fs24  caster:
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx2880\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\adjustright {\i\fs24 enlarge\tab invisibility*
\par fear*
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\adjustright {\fs24 
\par The following spells can be made permanent if cast on an object or area:
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx2880\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\adjustright {\i\fs24 alarm\tab prismatic sphere
\par audible glamer\tab solid fog
\par dancing lights\tab stinking cloud*
\par distance distortion\tab teleport*
\par enlarge\tab Von Gasik\rquote s refusal\'86
\par fear*\tab wall of fire
\par gust of wind\tab wall of force
\par magic mouth\tab web
\par Otiluke\rquote s dispelling screen\'87*
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\adjustright {\i\fs24 
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\adjustright {\fs24 \tab 
A permanent spell cast upon an object or area can be dispelled by any caster, but the dispel effect must be targeted solely upon the object or area carrying the permanent spell. A }{\i\fs24 dispel magic}{\fs24  c
ast against a permanent effect can only dispel one effect per casting. See the notes at the }{\i\fs24 dispel magic}{\fs24  entry for more information.
\par 
\par * See this section for further notes on this spell.
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 
\par Polymorph Any Object:}{\fs24  The DM usually must determine how long this lasts. If employed as a simple}{\i\fs24  polymorph other}{\fs24  or }{\i\fs24 stone to flesh}{\fs24 
 spell, the duration is permanent. If employed to turn a creature into an object or an object into another object, the duration is measured in hours or turns, as noted in the spell description.
\par }\pard\plain \s15\nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\adjustright {\tab 
Generally, the duration should not be less than two hours or turns. A change whose duration is measured in turns should not last more than a week, and a change whose duration is measured in hours will not last more than a day. The more radical the change,
 the shorter the duration. For example, turning a human into a teacup involves a change of kingdom (animal to mineral), plus a change in size and shape: This change might last 1d4+1 turns.
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\adjustright \fs20 {\fs24 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Polymorph Other:}{\fs24  This spell causes the target to assume the form of another creature of the caster\rquote 
s choosing. The caster cannot turn a creature into a plant or object. If the recipient fails the saving throw against the spell, there is an immediate system shock check to see if the creature survives the change. If the rec
ipient survives, the creature gains all the new form\rquote s purely physical abilities, but no abilities based on magic, agility, or intelligence. If the recipient\rquote s mentality changes to match the new form, the creature gains all the form\rquote 
s abilities.
\par 
\par \tab If the c
aster chooses a form that cannot survive under the local conditions, the recipient suffers 1d4 to 1d8 points of damage each day, hour, turn, or round it is exposed to such conditions. For example, a goldfish in a desert might suffer 1d8 points of damage e
v
ery round from heat and dryness. The same goldfish might suffer 1d6 points of damage every turn on a dungeon floor or 1d4 points of damage every day in a frigid mountain pool. Some creatures might be immune to environmental damage as long as their mentali
ty remains intact. For example, a mummy turned into a goldfish does not suffer from the desert heat.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Polymorph Self:}{\fs24  When the caster assumes a new form, the caster gains only the new form\rquote s normal mode of movement and breathing. The caster does not
 gain any special attacks or unusual abilities. The spell description uses the form of an owl as an example\emdash the caster gains the ability to fly but not an owl\rquote s extraordinary night vision (which is a special ability).
\par \tab When deciding what abilities are gained, the DM can immediately rule out any ability that does not arise from the form\rquote s physical characteristics. For example, a quickling\rquote 
s speed comes from its magically accelerated metabolism and is not derived purely from its physical form.
\par \tab In general, the DM should consider any non\_flying movement rate of greater than 24 or flying movement rate of greater than 36 as a special ability.
\par 
\par \tab The caster can assume the forms of creatures he has personally seen. For example, a caster who has never seen an ochre jelly cannot change into one.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Power Word, Stun:}{\fs24  Creatures affected by this spell are unable to take any meaningful actions. They cannot communicate, employ spells, use magical items, initiate psionic abilities, use spell\_
like abilities, fight, or move freely. Movement is limited to one third the creature\rquote s current movement rate, or a rate of 3, whichever is less. Attacks against stunned creatures gain a +4 bonus.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Protection from Evil:}{\fs24  Contrary to popular belief, this spell does not hedge out undead creatures (except ghouls, see the }{\i\fs24 Monstrous Manual}{\fs24 
 accessory) unless they have been brought to the scene by a conjuration/summoning spell (such as }{\i\fs24 monster summoning III}{\fs24 ) or have come from another plane.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Rope Trick:}{\fs24  A }{\i\fs24 rope trick}{\fs24  can support about 1,000 pounds, but the DM is free to assign a higher or lower limit. A frayed or rotten rope might break before the spell\rquote s limit is exceeded.
\par \tab Placing another extradimensional space inside the area created by a }{\i\fs24 rope trick}{\fs24  spell has catastrophic effects, see the note at extradimensional spaces in the magical items section (page 80).
\par \tab This spell is ineffective in the Astral Plane and in any locale where extradimensional spaces are inaccessible or nonexistent (see page 56). Creatures within the space created by a }{\i\fs24 rope trick}{\fs24 
 can breathe normally for the duration of the spell. 
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Shape Change:}{\fs24  This spell functions in much the same way as a }{\i\fs24 polymorph self}{\fs24  spell except that the caster can assume non\_animal forms and there are no size limitations. Unlike the }{\i\fs24 polymorph self}{\fs24 
 spell, the caster gains any ability the assumed form has provided the ability is not magical or mental in nature. For example, a character who changes into an owl gains its night vision, but changing into a cockatrice does not grant the monster\rquote 
s petrifying touch. The spell does not bestow magic resistance.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Stinking Cloud:}{\fs24  A permanent }{\i\fs24 stinking cloud}{\fs24  remains where it is created and generally is not disturbed by minor effects. If dispersed by a strong breeze or a }{\i\fs24 gust of wind}{\fs24  spel
l, the vapors return one round after the breeze or wind ceases. Even hurricane force winds cannot destroy the cloud, though the vapors are dispersed and ineffective while the winds last.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Stoneskin:}{\fs24  This spell is subject to considerable abuse by player characters. Multiple }{\i\fs24 stoneskins }{\fs24 placed on a single creature are not cumulative. If two or more }{\i\fs24 stoneskin}{\fs24 
 spells are cast on the same creature, roll normally for the number of attacks each spell protects against. If a new spell protects against more attacks th
an the present spell does, the recipient gets the benefit of the increased protection; otherwise there is no effect. The caster does not necessarily know how many attacks the spell can shield him from.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Stoneskin}{\fs24  protects only against blows, cuts, pokes, and slashes directed at the recipient. It does not protect against falls, magical attacks, touch\_delivered special attacks (such as touch\_
delivered spells, energy draining, green slime, etc.), or nonmagical attacks that do not involve blows (such as flaming oil, ingested or inhaled poisons, acid, constriction, and suffocation). }{\i\fs24 Stoneskin}{\fs24 
 lasts for 24 hours or until the spell has absorbed its allotment of attacks.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Teleport:}{\fs24  Regular use of this spell is very dangerous, as there is a slim chance that there can be an error even if the caster travels to well\_known locations. Additional notes regarding the definition of a well\_
known location are found under the }{\i\fs24 teleport without error }{\fs24 listing.
\par \tab Even minor alterations to a site can affect the caster\rquote s knowledge of a location. For example, Rozmare has spent many hours in her study, and the DM allows her to use the \ldblquote very familiar\rdblquote 
 category when determining how accurate her teleport spells are when her study is the destination. If a rival breaks in and rearranges the furniture, however, Rozmare\rquote s knowledge falls to \ldblquote studied carefully\rdblquote 
 or worse because she is not as familiar with the way things are currently arranged. If the intruder removed all the furniture and filled the study with boulders, Rozmare\rquote s knowledge falls to the \ldblquote never seen\rdblquote  category.
\par \tab Some players might attempt elaborate precautions to protect their characters from the disastrous effects of failed }{\i\fs24 teleport}{\fs24  spells; as the DM, you should not discourage such efforts, but keep the following in mind:
\par \tab A }{\i\fs24 teleport}{\fs24 
 spell requires a firm surface as a destination. The caster cannot choose to appear in the air or in a pool of water to avoid teleporting low. It is possible, however to teleport to a firm surface with a space or water underneath. If a teleporting charact
er arrives low, roll 1d100 to see how many yards below the surface the caster\rquote s feet land. Note that a low }{\i\fs24 teleport}{\fs24 
 is always fatal if the caster arrives within any solid object no matter how thin or flimsy the object is; teleporting low into a pile of feat
hers is just as deadly as teleporting into rock. Teleporting low into water is not immediately fatal, but the character still might drown if he can\rquote t hold his breath until he reaches the surface.
\par \tab A permanent }{\i\fs24 teleport}{\fs24  spell affects a single object with a volume of no more than 1,000 cubic feet (a 10\_
foot cube) or an area no larger than 400 square feet (20 feet square). The caster names the destination and rolls once for accuracy. The destination cannot be changed once set. The caster can assign a command word or non\_
verbal triggering device if he desires. This can be as simple or complex as the caster desires; see the }{\i\fs24 magic mouth}{\fs24  spell description in the }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Handbook}{\fs24 
 for limitations. If no command or trigger is set, anyone passing through the area or touching the object is teleported.
\par \tab Usually, only one creature can be teleported each round. It is possible to have several creatures teleport simultaneously provided they are touching the first creature to trigger the teleport and the additional creatures a
nd their equipment do not exceed the spell\rquote s weight limit, which is the same as the caster\rquote s weight limit at the time the original spell was cast.
\par 
\par If a permanent }{\i\fs24 teleport}{\fs24  spell is inaccurate, both the }{\i\fs24 permanency }{\fs24 and the }{\i\fs24 teleport}{\fs24 
 spell fail, but the caster can attempt a system shock roll to avoid losing a point of Constitution.  If this option is in play, the caster also might be allowed to set multiple destinations with the same permanent }{\i\fs24 teleport}{\fs24 
 spell.  The caster must name a different trigger for each destination and roll for accuracy each time a trigger is set.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Teleport Without Error:}{\fs24  As with}{\i\fs24  teleport}{\fs24 , this spell only allows travel to known locations. To know a location, the caster must learn what the place looks like or must
 be able to surmise where it is. For example, if the caster has been blindfold, carried into a chamber, then allowed to look around, the character could use}{\i\fs24  teleport without error}{\fs24 
 to return to the chamber even though he has no idea where the chamber is. The caster also could }{\i\fs24 teleport without error}{\fs24 
 into the courtyard of a castle visible in the distance even if he had never seen the courtyard before. Note that in both cases the caster could employ a normal }{\i\fs24 teleport}{\fs24  spell, but the caster\rquote s lack of knowledge ab
out the destination would make the attempt dangerous.
\par \tab A}{\i\fs24  teleport without error}{\fs24  spell has no chance for error if the destination lies in the same world as the caster. If the caster accidentally specifies a destination already occupied by a solid object, th
e character is automatically displaced a sufficient distance to allow for a safe arrival.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Teleport}{\fs24  }{\i\fs24 without error}{\fs24  also allows travel between world (planes, crystal spheres, and pocket dimensions) but there is a chance for error, see the spell description for details.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Unseen Servant: }{\fs24 
The force this spell creates does not possess any senses or powers of reason. It is incapable of any action except following its instructions to the letter. For example, an unseen servant can be sent to the bottom of a pool to 
grab whatever objects it encounters, but it cannot be directed to grab any coins or gems that it finds.
\par \tab The permanent version of this spell creates an invisible servant that always hovers within 30 feet of the caster. If destroyed, it reforms in 2d10 rounds.
\par 
\par \tab A permanent }{\i\fs24 unseen servant }{\fs24 can be cast on an area and instructed to endlessly perform a single task, such as forever cleaning a room. Once a task is set, it cannot be changed.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Web: }{\i\fs24 Webs }{\fs24 must be properly supported if they are to be made permanent. Pe
rmanent webbing remains where it is created, if torn away, new webs spring into existence to take its place. Chunks of webbing carried out of the area of effect dissolve in seconds. Creatures can break through the webbing at the rates given in the spell d
escription, but the webs immediately fill in behind them. Creatures who blunder into permanent webbing (or who are thrown in) can be trapped and suffocated if they fail to save vs. spell just as they can if caught in normal webbing.
\par \tab Permanent }{\i\fs24 webs }{\fs24 can be burned away with fire, but they spring back into being one round after the flames die away.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Wish:}{\fs24  Most uses of this spell lower the caster\rquote s Strength/Stamina score by three points and force the caster to take to his bed for 2d4 days. Lost Strength/Stamina returns at the end of the rest period. If the caster\rquote 
s Strength score falls to 0 or less, he loses consciousness until he has rested. 
\par \tab A }{\i\fs24 wish}{\fs24  essentially allows the user to change reality to suit his tastes. The alteration, however, can have unintended co
nsequences, especially if the wish is poorly worded or the caster gets greedy. Generally, the more local and personal the effect, the less chance there is for complications. Adjudicating this spell is tricky, as the DM must be sure to give the players res
ults that reflect the power of the }{\i\fs24 wish}{\fs24 , but not so much that the players come to rely on }{\i\fs24 wishes }{\fs24 to solve all their problems. A }{\i\fs24 wish }{\fs24 can always duplicate any spell of 9th level or less.
\par \tab To avoid the bed rest and Strength/Stamina loss associated with a }{\i\fs24 wish }{\fs24 spell, the caster must effect a change that does not leave him better off than before the events that preceded the }{\i\fs24 wish}{\fs24 
. For example, if the wizard\rquote s party was defeated by a powerful monster, the caster could }{\i\fs24 wish }{\fs24 that they had never met the creature.
 This change in reality brings his companions back to life as if the encounter had never occurred. The caster ages five years, but he does not have to rest for 2d4 days from the }{\i\fs24 wish }{\fs24 
since he is not in a better position than before the events occurred.
\par \tab If
 the caster wished for his party to return but the monster to remain dead, he would be subject to the debilitative effects, since the creature being dead is considered an advantage that the spellcaster did not have before the events occurred. Any time a }
{\i\fs24 wish }{\fs24 creates an advantage for the caster the loss of Strength and the 2d4 days of bed rest occurs.
\par \tab The effective power of a }{\i\fs24 wish }{\fs24 is based upon the availability of money and magical items in your campaign world. If these are in abundance, the power of the wi
sh is enhanced. Likewise, in a world where money and magical items are scarce, the power of a wish is reduced. As DM, you need to determine the relative power of a }{\i\fs24 wish }{\fs24 
in your world. Here are a few guidelines for a world that has a moderate amount of wealth and magical items:
\par \tab A }{\i\fs24 wish }{\fs24 can produce a magical item but not an artifact. To avoid suffering bed rest and Strength loss, the caster should place a limit on the length of time the item is kept, typically about one hour. The item isn\rquote 
t actually created, it\rquote s just borrowed and goes back where it came from when the duration expires. If the item is particularly rare or valuable, or has been borrowed before, the true owner might resent the loan.
\par \tab A }{\i\fs24 wish }{\fs24 can bring the user wealth. The DM should decide how much a character can }{\i\fs24 wish}{\fs24 
 for without trouble. The amount gained should be significant but not so great as to disrupt the game. For most campaigns, a random amount of 5,000 to 40,000 gp (5d8x1,000) shouldn\rquote t cause problems.
\par \tab A }{\i\fs24 wish }{\fs24 can change a character\rquote s race permanently, allowing an elf to become a human and advance without level limitations. Alternatively, that same elf could }{\i\fs24 wish }{\fs24 
to advance in level like a human, but he could at most gain one level per }{\i\fs24 wish}{\fs24 . Each time he wanted to advance in level, he would have to cast another }{\i\fs24 wish }{\fs24 to allow it to occur.
\par \tab A }{\i\fs24 wish }{\fs24 can usually negate or change events that the user finds undesirable\emdash this is why }{\i\fs24 wishes}{\fs24  are part of the AD&D game. A }{\i\fs24 wish }{\fs24 used to alter a campaign\rquote s history should be immediate
\emdash made on the spot or very soon after the event to be altered took place. }{\i\fs24 Wishes }{\fs24 
that allow player characters a second chance to achieve a goal after an unlucky failure or disastrous mistake should be allowed, as long as the terms of the wish don\rquote t guarantee success.
\par \tab When assigning consequences to poorly worded or inappropriate }{\i\fs24 wishes}{\fs24 , it is best to follow two guidelines: First, the errant }{\i\fs24 wish}{\fs24  should follow the player\rquote 
s wording to the letter. Second, the result should follow the path of least resistance; that is, the result should involve the simplest and least complex warping of reality. For example, a greedy character who tries to }{\i\fs24 wish}{\fs24  for a }{
\i\fs24 staff of the magi}{\fs24  might very well find himself standing naked and alone, staff in hand, in front of the staff\rquote s former owner (perhaps a dragon or lich). Escaping from the former owner and returning home is the character\rquote 
s problem.
\par \tab As with }{\i\fs24 limited wish}{\fs24 , the unnatural aging caused by the spell is dependent upon the race of the caster; five years for a human, 10 years for a halfling or half\_el
f, 15 years for a dwarf, 20 years for a gnome, and 25 years for an elf. The aging requires a system shock roll, and failure results in death for the caster.
\par \tab Chapter 1 of the }{\i\fs24 Dungeon Master Guide}{\fs24  discusses the effects of }{\i\fs24 wishes }{\fs24 on ability scores, and additional information can also be found in }{\i\fs24 The Complete Wizard\rquote s Handbook. 
\par }{\fs24 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Priest Spells}{\fs24 
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Age Creature\'86:}{\fs24  The reverse of this spell, }{\i\fs24 restore youth}{\fs24 , negates most sorts of magical aging, provided the aging is the magic\rquote s primary effect. It negates aging from }{\i\fs24 age creature }{\fs24 spells, 
}{\i\fs24 staffs of withering}{\fs24 , and attacks by ghosts. It does not reverse incidental aging effects, such as those inflicted by casting a }{\i\fs24 wish}{\fs24  or receiving a}{\i\fs24  haste}{\fs24  spell.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Breath of Life\'86:}{\fs24  The reverse of this spell, }{\i\fs24 breath of death}{\fs24 , produces a nonmagical disease that }{\i\fs24 breath of life}{\fs24  can cure.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Combine:}{\fs24  The central priest gains a boost to the spells and granted abilities he already has. The central priest gains no extra spells or granted abilities from this spell.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Dispel Evil: }{\fs24 In addition to driving away evil extra\_planar and summoned creatures, this spell is effective against evil enchantment/charm spells and all forms of domination and possession.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Dispel Magic: }{\fs24 Refer to the wizard\rquote s version of this spell.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Dragonbane\'86: }{\fs24 This spell can be the subject of a site focus\'86.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Draw Upon Holy Might\'86: }{\fs24 This spell cannot increase an ability score beyond 25.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Extradimensional Pocket\'86: }{\fs24 The extradimensional space created by this spell functions as a }{\i\fs24 bag of holding}{\fs24  in all respects while its duration lasts.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Imbue with Spell Ability: }{\fs24 If the recipient dies before the imbued spells are cast, the }{\i\fs24 imbue with spell ability}{\fs24  caster regains the ability to cast the imbued spells.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Know Time\'86: }{\fs24 This spell reveals the correct local time in terms the caster can most readily under
stand. If the caster has just arrived on a new world where he is unfamiliar with the names of hours, days, months, and years, the spell reveals a generic result that might not be immediately useful until the caster gets more information. For example, the 
spell might reveal that it is the 10th hour of the 23rd day of the 7th month in the 2,345th year. The hour is always given in relation to local midnight.
\par \tab If the world where the }{\i\fs24 know time }{\fs24 spell is cast has a time flow different from that of the bas
e campaign, this spell has a 2% chance per caster level of giving an estimation of the difference. The caster can learn if time flows faster or slower and the general degree of difference; great, moderate, or minor. When using table 2 (page 46), ratings o
f 2\endash 4 and 18\endash 20 are great; ratings of 5\endash 7 and 15\endash 17 are moderate, and ratings of 8\endash 9 and 13\endash 14 are minor.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Magic Font: }{\fs24 This spell requires a specially prepared font for creating holy water (see page 96). The spell\rquote s maximum duration depends on the font\rquote s capacity, but the actual time the caster can scry depends on the caster
\rquote s knowledge of the subject, as given in the }{\i\fs24 crystal ball}{\fs24  description in the }{\i\fs24 Dungeon Master Guide}{\fs24 . For example, a magic font spell cast on a basin with a capacity of 60 vials remains acti
ve for one hour, but the actual time the caster can safely use the font is 30 minutes if the subject being viewed is known slightly.
\par \tab Several other spells can make a }{\i\fs24 magic font}{\fs24  more useful, see the }{\i\fs24 crystal ball}{\fs24  description in the }{\i\fs24 DMG}{\fs24  for the list. See the notes on the }{\i\fs24 magic mirror}{\fs24  and }{\i\fs24 clairvoyance}{
\fs24  spells for more information on scrying.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Mind Read\'86:}{\fs24  This spell functions just like the wizard spell }{\i\fs24 ESP}{\fs24  in most respects. Each time a }{\i\fs24 mind read}{\fs24  spell is cast, however, the priest can conduct a deep probe of a sin
gle creature, possibly gaining additional information as detailed in the spell description.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Mistaken Missive\'86:}{\fs24  This spell can affect any document written in ink. For purposes of the spell, ink is
 any substance that is artificially compounded or altered to render it suitable for use in writing. Documents written with substances that have not been artificially prepared are not subject to this spell. For example, a note written in chalk cannot be al
tered by this spell, neither can a letter or agreement written in blood.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Music of the Spheres\'86:}{\fs24  A successful saving throw against this spell negates only the entrancing effect. An opponent who successfully saves is free to attack the caster but still suffers the \endash 3 penalty to charm\_
based saving throws for as long as he can hear the music.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Nap\'86:}{\fs24  This spell does not reduce the study time (10 minutes per spell level) required to memorize spells. The spell has no effect if the recipient is unwilling or has received a }{\i\fs24 nap}{\fs24 
 spell in the previous 18 hours.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Plane Shift:}{\fs24  This spell sends the caster and up to seven other creatures on a one\_way trip to another plane. This spell also allows travel between crystal spheres on the Prime Material Plane (though conditions w
ithin a particular sphere might prevent the spell from working). Each sphere requires a unique forked rod made of metal, just as each plane or dimension does. The travelers can return home via a second }{\i\fs24 plane shift }{\fs24 
spell if they have a rod attuned to their home plane or world.
\par 
\par \tab Two\_way travel is possible with a single }{\i\fs24 plane shift }{\fs24 
spell if the DM chooses to allow it. To return home without a second spell, the travelers need the same rod that was used in the original spell, and they must be on the same plane as their original destination. For example, a group of travelers who }{
\i\fs24 plane shift}{\fs24  to the Outlands and then pass through a gate to the Abyss cannot use the original rod to return home unless they return to the Outlands first. They also cannot return home without another spell if they lose the original rod.

\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Acquiring rods:}{\fs24  When a priest gains access to this spell, he usually discovers the type of rod required to reach his home world and to reach the plane where his deity resides. The DM must decide how easy o
r difficult it is to discover additional rods. The surest way to obtain a rod attuned to a specific plane is to find a priest who has been there before. Otherwise, the priest must conduct his own research to discover what sort of rod is required to reach 
a particular place. The table of suggested costs assumes that planar travel is intended to be fairly rare, but not unknown. The DM should adjust costs up or down as appropriate.
\par \tab The priest must be in good heath and refrain from adventuring while researching a rod. If the priest has access to }{\i\fs24 commune }{\fs24 
spells, the required research time is reduced one step (one year of research time is reduced to one month), but costs are not reduced. At the end of the research time, the priest must attempt a Wisdom/Intuition 
check. If failed, the research is unsuccessful but may be conducted again. If the check succeeds, the priest discovers the type of rod required to reach the plane he was researching; the priest knows the rod\rquote 
s shape and what materials are required to make it. Finding the materials and a craftsman to make the rod are another problem.
\par \tab The DM is free to decide what rods look like (there are many possible objects that can be described as forked rods). See volume two of the }{\i\fs24 Encyclopedia Magica }{\fs24 for examples.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Protection from Evil: }{\fs24 Refer to the wizard version of this spell.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Reflecting Pool:}{\fs24  This spell requires a natural pool\emdash a small body of water fed by a natural water source and contained in a setting generally free of artificial constructions. A naturally occur
ring puddle of rainwater could be considered a pool if it lies in a meadow but not if it lies in a city street.
\par \tab See the notes on the wizard spells }{\i\fs24 magic mirror }{\fs24 and }{\i\fs24 clairvoyance }{\fs24 for more information on how this spell functions.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Speak With Dead: }{\fs24 This spell has a range of one yard. The dead do not lie, but they can be evasive, misleading, or obtusely literal if they answer the caster\rquote 
s questions at all (some creatures are allowed saving throws, see the spell description).
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Unceasing Vigilance of the Holy Sentinel\'86: }{\fs24 
A priest recovering from this spell must rest unless compelled to act by some external cause. Generally, the priest cannot respond to threats that he cannot perceive (though the priest always perceives a threat to himself if he suffers damage). A }{
\i\fs24 nap}{\fs24  spell grants the priest 48 turns of rest.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Weighty Chest\'86: }{\fs24 The weight increase created by this spell is activated only when a creature other than the caster attempts to move or lift the protected chest. It is not possible to use a }{\i\fs24 weighty chest }{\fs24 as a wea
pon. For example, the caster cannot cast this spell on a small coffer and then toss it at an opponent, hoping the foe will be bowled over or unbalanced by the coffer\rquote 
s great weight. Note, however, that a foe could be tricked into attempting to lift or move the chest.
\par 
