\par }{\fs24\super 1}{\fs24   The Astral and Ethereal Planes are treated as known Inner Planes for purposes of research.
\par 
\par }{\fs24\super 2}{\fs24   The numbers before the slashes are the cost and time requirements for planes that are generally known by the campaign\rquote 
s spellcasters. The numbers after the slash are the cost and time requirements for destinations about which little is known in the home campaign. All prices are in gold pieces.
\par 
\par }{\fs24\super 3}{\fs24   The number is the typical cost in gold pieces for constructing one rod, provided that the proper materials are available. Rods made of extremely rare materials can cost considerably more.
\par 
\par }{\fs24\super 4}{\fs24   Add these modifiers to the type of plane the Pocket Dimension is attached to. For example, researching a Pock
et Dimension that is attached to the Ethereal Plane would cost 2,500 gp and take five weeks. The cost for the rod would remain 100 gp.
\par 
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Magical Items}{\fs24 
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Amulet of Life Protection:}{\fs24 
 A character whose psyche is held in the amulet does not truly die until seven days have passed. Until that time, any healing the character receives revives the character as long as the healing is sufficient to give the character a positive hit point tota
l.
\par \tab The wearer can be raised or resurrected no matter how the character died. The }{\i\fs24 raise dead}{\fs24  or }{\i\fs24 resurrection}{\fs24  spells can be cast upon the amulet if the character\rquote s body has been destroyed.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Bag of Holding:}{\fs24  Living creatures can be placed within a }{\i\fs24 bag of holding}{\fs24  provided they don\rquote t exceed the bag\rquote 
s volume and weight restrictions. If the bag is left open, living creatures kept inside can breathe normally. The space inside a }{\i\fs24 bag of holding}{\fs24  is airtight, and if the bag is sealed, the air inside runs out
 quickly. It is possible to carry water in a }{\i\fs24 bag of holding}{\fs24 . See the general note under extradimensional spaces for more information.
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx2160\tx3960\tx7200\adjustright {\b\fs24 Bag Capacity\tab Air*\tab Water**}{\fs24 
\par 250 lbs.\tab 4 minutes\tab 30 gallons
\par 500 lbs.\tab 6 minutes\tab 60 gallons
\par 1,000 lbs.\tab 8 minutes\tab 120 gallons
\par 1,500 lbs.\tab 10 minutes\tab 180 gallons
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\adjustright {\fs24 
\par * This is the amount of time a single creature in a sealed bag remains comfortable. After the listed time, the air becomes foul and the creature begins gasping; a \endash 2 penalty applies to all attack rolls and ability checks un
til the creature gets fresh air. If the creature remains in the bag for twice the listed time, it must save vs. poison or fall unconscious until the creature gets fresh air. The save must be repeated each turn. Unconscious creatures also must save vs. poi
son every turn, and they die if they fail a second time.
\par ** This shows the amount of water the bag can hold. Note that water is heavy and a bag carrying the listed amount of water only appears to be about 10% full as far as its cubic capacity is concerned. This makes it very easy to exceed the bag\rquote 
s weight limit and destroy it.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Books/Tomes: }{\fs24 The baneful effects from all books, tomes, manuals, and librams are triggered by perusing even a small passage. Magical books cannot be distinguished from other types of normal or magical books.
\par \tab A character who studies a book to find out what\rquote s in it triggers the book\rquote s effects. Magical books always vanish once they bestow a beneficial effect but usually remain behind if they inflict a harmful effect. Multi\_classed chara
cters get only the best possible result\emdash other helpful results (and harmful ones) are ignored. For example, an elf fighter/mage/thief glances at a }{\i\fs24 manual of puissant skill at arms}{\fs24 
, a book that is normally harmful to wizards. Because the elf is a fighter, he can ignore the harmful effect and gain one fighter level instead.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Cloak of Displacement:}{\fs24  The cloak\rquote s displacement power is ineffective against creatures or devices that cannot see the cloak\rquote s wearer. For example, an invisible character does not receive the cloak\rquote 
s power to make opponents miss their initial attack or the cloak\rquote s armor class bonus; likewise, most traps never \ldblquote see\rdblquote  their targets and displacement does not foil them.
\par \tab Displacement is not effective against attacks that are not aimed, such as an avalanche or cave\_in, and does not affect aimed attacks that cover an area, such as catapult shots or dragon tail slaps.
\par \tab Displacement is only partially effective against spell attacks. If a spell actually requires an attack roll, such as any touch\_delive
red spell, the cloak works normally and can cause the spell attack to miss if it is the first attack in an encounter. If the attacking spell does not require an attack roll, it can never be caused to \ldblquote miss,\rdblquote  though the cloak\rquote 
s +2 saving throw bonus applies. 
\par \tab For example a }{\i\fs24 fireball}{\fs24  spell never misses, but the cloak wearer gains a +2 saving throw bonus. If a spell allows no saving throw, displacement has no effect on it; for example, a }{\i\fs24 magic missile}{\fs24  or }{\i\fs24 
death spell}{\fs24  is never affected by displacement.
\par \tab Under normal conditions, the first melee or missile attack against a displaced creature automatically misses. The opponent is assumed to note the displaced creature\rquote 
s correct position and can keep track of it thereafter. If an opponent has multiple attacks, only t
he first one automatically misses. If there are multiple opponents, only the first attack by the first creature automatically misses. The remaining opponents are assumed to observe the failed attack and make the appropriate adjustments. If the DM determin
e
s that one or more creatures did not observe the initial attack, their first attacks automatically miss, too. Note that intelligent opponents who have reason to suspect a character is displaced might launch some type of probing attack to test the characte
r\rquote s defenses, such as hurling a rock. Such attacks count as a combat action for the creatures attempting them.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Contract of Nepthas:}{\fs24  A }{\i\fs24 mistaken missive}{\fs24  spell alters the words written on the a }{\i\fs24 contract of Nepthas}{\fs24  but does not free characters who have signed the contract from their obligations.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Crystal Balls:}{\fs24  See the notes under the }{\i\fs24 magic mirror}{\fs24  and }{\i\fs24 clairaudience }{\fs24 spells.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Daern\rquote s Instant Fortress:}{\fs24  A creature attacking the fortress\rquote s walls with a magical weapon inflicts one point of damage for every three rounds spent attacking the walls. If the escalade rules from the }{\i\fs24 Player
\rquote s Option: Combat and Tactics}{\fs24  book are in play, the fortress can be attacked by bombardment engines or sapped by attackers equipped with magical weapons. In either case, all damage is subtracted from the fortress\rquote s total hit points.

\par 
\par Deck of Many Things: Baneful effects from this item cannot be removed through }{\i\fs24 wishes}{\fs24  or lesser means, although a }{\i\fs24 wish}{\fs24  might indirectly help the PCs in dealing with the difficulties the cards inflict. For example, a }{
\i\fs24 wish}{\fs24  can reveal where a victim of the void or the donjon is imprisoned. A }{\i\fs24 wish}{\fs24  also could reveal the identity of an enemy produced by the flames or the rogue.
\par \tab 10th\_level magic is effective against a }{\i\fs24 deck of many things}{\fs24  in the same way a }{\i\fs24 wish}{\fs24  is, but there are certain exceptions. 10th\_
level divination spells cannot be used to determine the identity of a particular card nor can magical wards negate a card\rquote s effe
cts, but they can reveal the location of a creature trapped by the void or donjon card. There is no way to shield a creature from the harmful effects of the }{\i\fs24 deck of many things}{\fs24  while allowing the benefits to occur by using 10th\_
level spells.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Extradimensional Spaces:}{\fs24  These items tend to produce spectacular effects when one is placed within another. The following items contain extradimensional spaces: }{\i\fs24 bag of holding, bag of transmuting, flatbox\'86
, girdle of many pouches, Heward\rquote s handy haversack, portable hole}{\fs24 , and }{\i\fs24 pouch of accessibility}{\fs24 . The following spells produce extradimensional spaces: }{\i\fs24 extradimensional pocket\'86, Mordenkainen\rquote 
s magnificent mansion}{\fs24 , and }{\i\fs24 rope trick}{\fs24 .
\par \tab In most instances, placing one extradimensional space inside another opens a rift to the Ast
ral Plane, casting both the items and their contents through the rift. The items and anything contained within them are scattered randomly in the infinite depths of the Astral Plane. A }{\i\fs24 wish}{\fs24 
 can recover the contents of the extradimensional spaces, and it is
 possible that creatures held in the items might eventually find their way off the Astral Plane. Since all objects within the extradimensional space are scattered randomly, a creature cast into the Astral Plane through a rift does not have any better chan
ce of recovering lost items than any other creature. A creature carried to the Astral Plane through a rift retains its possessions, but other loose items within the extradimensional space are randomly scattered. 
\par \tab For example, a party of adventurers decides to cast a }{\i\fs24 rope trick}{\fs24  spell to create a safe haven where they can rest and sort a huge pile of coins they have found. Unfortunately, one of the characters has a }{\i\fs24 bag of holding}{
\fs24  which contains several pieces of equipment and treasure. When the}{\i\fs24  bag of holding}{\fs24  enters the }{\i\fs24 rope trick}{\fs24 , both spaces are sucked into the Astral Plane. The characters occupying the}{\i\fs24  rope trick}{\fs24 
 are dumped in random locations in the Astral Plane (if the DM is feeling kind, they might arrive within sight of each other). The}{\i\fs24  bag of holding}{\fs24  is torn from its holder\rquote 
s grasp and its contents are spewed randomly across astral space.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Portable holes}{\fs24  can produce more dramatic effects. If another extradimensional space is placed within a }{\i\fs24 portable hole}{\fs24 , an astral rift opens, as described above. However, if a }{\i\fs24 portable hole}{\fs24 
 is placed within another extradimensional space, a gate to a random plane opens and all creatures within a 10\_foot radius are drawn through it, no saving throw. The process destroys the }{\i\fs24 portable hole}{\fs24  and the other extradimensional spa
ce.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Flatboxes}{\fs24  are notoriously unstable. If a }{\i\fs24 flatbox }{\fs24 contacts any other extradimensional space it explodes, see the item description for details. The other item is sucked into the Astral Plane. A }{\i\fs24 
portable hole}{\fs24  reacts as detailed above.
\par \tab Most extradimensional spaces contain only a finite amount of air, which limits how long living creatures can be kept inside. Refer to the }{\i\fs24 bag of holding}{\fs24  entry for the amount of air contained within these items.
\par 
\par \tab Creatures drawn through the gate created by a }{\i\fs24 portable hole}{\fs24  arrive in a random location and fall in a heap within a 10\_
foot radius. Items in the extradimensional spaces are either lost on the Astral Plane (50%) or scattered randomly about the circle where the creatures land (50%). The DM makes the roll and can decide to check the items singly or in groups.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Flatbox:}{\fs24  The box can hold 60 gallons of water. A creature inside one of these items can breathe normally for six minutes if the lid is closed. Additional information can be found at the }{\i\fs24 bag of holding }{\fs24 
and extradimensional spaces entries.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Flight Items: }{\fs24 Characters using magical items that grant flight have a daily movement rate in miles equal to twice the item\rquote s flight speed. For example, characters aboard a 4\_person }{\i\fs24 carpet of flying }{\fs24 
travel 48 miles a day in clear weather. 
\par \tab The daily movement rate assumes 10 hours of flying time with ample rest stops; it is not an altogether pleasant experience to fly (consider the effects of rough air, unsteady seating, awkward body positioning, and exposure to we
ather). Characters in a hurry can eliminate most rest periods and stay aloft longer, spending 18\endash 20 hours in the air and doubling the daily movement rate, but this subjects the riders to the effects of a forced march (see }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote 
s Handbook}{\fs24 , Chapter 14). Riders who stay aloft for 24 hours a day move at 2 1/2 times their normal daily rate and suffer double force march penalties.
\par \tab When a party has access to magical items that grant flight, the DM should take special care to plan adventures that take this capability into account. Flying characters can easily evade most land\_
based encounters, so the adventure should include encounters with flying creatures or those that entice the characters to land. The DM should also determine the prevailing weather conditions in advance, as they affect both flying conditions and the party
\rquote s ability to see and be seen while aloft.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Gem of Insight:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 A character can benefit from one of these items only once, no matter how many gems are found over the course of the character\rquote s lifetime or how long a single }{\i\fs24 gem of insight}{\fs24 
 is kept.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Girdle of Many Pouches:}{\fs24  Though intended to hold equipment, this item\rquote s small pouches can hold about one gallon of water or a single tiny creature. If belted around a character\rquote s waist, the pouches are seale
d and the creatures inside them have about four minutes of air. Additional information can be found at the }{\i\fs24 bag of holding}{\fs24  and extradimensional spaces entries.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Heward\rquote s Handy Haversack:}{\fs24  Though intended to hold equipment, this item\rquote s compartments can hold w
ater or creatures. The side pouches can hold two gallons of water or one tiny creature each. The central compartment can hold eight gallons of water or a single small creature. If strapped around a character\rquote 
s back and closed, creatures inside the haversack have about four minutes of air. Additional information can be found at the }{\i\fs24 bag of holding}{\fs24  and extradimensional spaces entries.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Iron Bands of Bilarro:}{\fs24  There is no saving throw against this item, though the user must make a successful attack roll to trap a target. A failed attack roll never entraps a creature. An entrapped victim\rquote 
s companions can attempt a bend bars/lift gates roll to break the bands if the victim cannot get free. Spells such as}{\i\fs24  free action, wraithform, antimagic shell}{\fs24 , and }{\i\fs24 duo\_dimension}{\fs24  a
re all effective means of escape, but teleportation magic merely transports the trapped creature from one place to another with the bands still trapping him. Magical items such as a }{\i\fs24 potion of slipperiness}{\fs24  or }{\i\fs24 ring of free action
}{\fs24  are also effective against the magic of the bands.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Javelin of Lightning:}{\fs24  This item has a maximum range of 90 yards. The lightning created is a single bolt that extends from the target toward the thrower. Thus, a javelin of lightning should not be used if the target is within 30 feet.

\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Librams and Manuals:}{\fs24  See note at books.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Medallion of ESP:}{\fs24  Refer to the wizard spell }{\i\fs24 ESP}{\fs24 .
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Mirror of Mental Prowess:}{\fs24  This item\rquote s thought\_reading power works just like the wizard spell }{\i\fs24 ESP}{\fs24 . See the notes at the wizard spells }{\i\fs24 clairvoyance, clairaudience,}{\fs24  and }{\i\fs24 magic mirror}
{\fs24  for information on the mirror\rquote s scrying powers.
\par \tab Travel through the portal created by the mirror is instantaneous. A }{\i\fs24 detect invisibility}{\fs24  or }{\i\fs24 true seeing}{\fs24  spell reveals the portal.
\par \tab The mirror\rquote s power to answer a question each week is similar to the priest spell }{\i\fs24 commune}{\fs24  in most respects, but the user is limited to questions about a creature whose reflection is being cast in the mirror.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Periapt of Proof Against Poison:}{\fs24  This item has three basic functions, but only one can be active at any given time. First, the periapt can allow a saving throw ag
ainst poisons that normally do not allow one. The required number for the saving throw varies with the periapt\rquote s strength as shown in the }{\i\fs24 Dungeon Master Guide}{\fs24 
. Other magical protections are added to the roll. For example, a character with a }{\i\fs24 periapt of proof against poison +1}{\fs24  and a}{\i\fs24  ring of protection +1}{\fs24 
 would gain a saving throw of 18 against a toxin that normally allows no saving throw. The bonus for the periapt does not apply in cases where no saving throw is normally allowed.
\par \tab Second, the periapt negates any penalty a particularly strong toxin might impose. Note that the penalty is entirely negated, not merely subtracted from the periapt\rquote s bonus. For example, a particularly virulent poison might have a \endash 
4 penalty to all saving throws. Even a }{\i\fs24 periapt of proof against poison +1}{\fs24  completely negates the penalty. Likewise, a poison with a \endash 1 penalty to saving throws completely negates the bonus of a +4 periapt.
\par \tab Third, the periapt grants a general bonus to normal saving throws against poisons. The bonuses are cumulative with other magical protections (but see the automatic failure rule on page 142).
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Portable Hole:}{\fs24  This item has a capacity of about 280 cubic feet. It has no weight limit, and about 2,100 gallons of water or 100,000 standard coins can be held inside. A creature in a}{\i\fs24  portable hole}{\fs24 
 has enough air for 10 minutes. See the note at }{\i\fs24 bag of holding}{\fs24  for the effects of depleted air, and see the general note on extradimensional spaces for more information.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Potions:}{\fs24  Once a potion, elixir, oil, or ointment takes effect on a creature, any effects that apply only to the imbiber cannot be removed unless a }{\i\fs24 dispel magic}{\fs24  is targeted directly at the creature. If the potion
\rquote s effects extend to other creatures (such as the various potions of control) it can be dispelled normally. All potion effects are treated as magic cast at 12th level for purposes of dispelling.
\par \tab Potions consumed within an }{\i\fs24 antimagic shell}{\fs24  do not activate until they leave the area of effect. If a potion has been consumed, an }{\i\fs24 antimagic shell}{\fs24  suppresses it
s effects unless they are permanent in nature (such as a }{\i\fs24 potion of healing}{\fs24 ). Temporary effects made permanent by a roll on the potion compatibility table from the DMG can be suppressed by an }{\i\fs24 antimagic shell}{\fs24 .
\par \tab Tasting a potion gives the character a minor clue as to the potion\rquote s effects. For example, a }{\i\fs24 potion of levitation}{\fs24  or a }{\i\fs24 potion of flying}{\fs24 
 might make the character feel light. Often the effect from tasting a potion is not immed
iately obvious, and the character must attempt some action before any effects are revealed. In the previous example, the taster might feel nothing initially but might walk with a bouncing gait or feel light\_footed when walking. A potion\rquote 
s taste, smell, and texture might help identify it, but this tends to be unreliable because potions with identical effects can look, feel, smell, and taste differently if they were made in different laboratories or concocted at different times.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Potion of Vitality:}{\fs24  A character drinking this potion increases his body\rquote s natural healing ability to the rate of one hit point recovered every four hours. Damage that cannot be healed by magical means\emdash such as from a }{
\i\fs24 sword of wounding}{\fs24 \emdash is restored. Damage that can be healed only by magical means\emdash such as wounds from a chasme tanar\rquote ri\rquote s claws or the fists of a clay golem\emdash is not restored.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Pouch of Accessibility:}{\fs24  Though intended to hold equipment, this item\rquote s internal pouches can hold about one gallon of water or a single tiny creature. Clo
sing the pouch seals all the internal compartments, and the creatures inside them have about four minutes of air. See the note at}{\i\fs24  bag of holding}{\fs24  for the effects of depleted air, and refer to extradimensional spaces for more information.

\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Quiver of Ehlonna:}{\fs24  Only long, thin objects such as arrows, javelins, and bows can be placed in this item. Creatures cannot be placed inside, nor does the quiver hold water.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Ring of Blinking:}{\fs24  See note at the wizard spell }{\i\fs24 blink}{\fs24 .
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Ring of Contrariness:}{\fs24  This cursed item always makes the wearer do things that run counter to what others desire. The wearer does not necessarily do the exact opposite of what is suggested. For example, if someone says \ldblquote 
keep that ring on,\rdblquote  the wearer wholeheartedly agrees. He might also suddenly become fearful that others desire the ring and attack the speaker. The ring\rquote 
s enchantment makes the wearer difficult to be around, always selecting the response that is most troublesome.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Ring of Regeneration:}{\fs24  Wearers killed by fire, acid, disintegration, or death magic cannot regenerate back to life. However, damage inflicted by such attacks can be regenerated if the wearer survives the attack.
\par \tab A }{\i\fs24 ring of regeneration}{\fs24  only repairs damage inflicted on the wearer after the character puts on the ring. Damage inflicted before the character wore the ring is not regenerated, so placing a }{\i\fs24 ring of regeneration}{\fs24 
 on a dead or unconscious character has no effect.
\par \tab A }{\i\fs24 ring of regeneration}{\fs24  does not remove the need to eat, sleep, or breathe, nor does it prevent natural or unnatural aging.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Rod of Absorption:}{\fs24  Spell levels stored in the rod can be used to power spells on low\_magic worlds, even when local conditions would not normally allow the spell to be cast. For example, if the rod\_wielder had a }{\i\fs24 wall of 
force}{\fs24  spell memorized, the character could use five levels of stored energy even on an M4 world, where 5th level spells normally don\rquote t work (see page 47).
\par \tab When used to absorb spells, the rod can absorb any spells directed at the wielder for the entire round, as selected by the rod\_
wielder. Absorbing spells counts as an action for the character, but initiative has no bearing on when a spell can be absorbed. The wielder can never absorb a spell that is not targeted specifically at him. 
\par \tab For example, if the wielder is caught in the blast of a }{\i\fs24 fireball}{\fs24 , the rod cannot be used to absorb the spell because the wielder was not the target\emdash the actual target was a point in space. If, however, the }{\i\fs24 fireball}{
\fs24  was set to detonate directly on the wielder, it could be absorbed. Some spells, such as }{\i\fs24 hold person}{\fs24  and }{\i\fs24 slow}{\fs24 , are individually targeted on multiple creatures within an area. If the rod\_
wielder is one of those targets, he can absorb the entire spell.
\par \tab Absorbed spells have no effect whatsoever; their power has been stored in the rod. Thus, if a}{\i\fs24  hold person}{\fs24  is directed at the rod\_wielder, the magic is totally negated\emdash even for other targets. 10th\_
level spells cannot be absorbed.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Rod of Beguiling:}{\fs24  The beguiling effect has a 20\_foot radius. There is no saving throw, though
 magic resistance applies, as does resistance to mental attacks or control. Racial resistances to charm effects also apply. Affected creatures remain beguiled for the full one\_turn duration even if they leave the radius.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Rod of Resurrection:}{\fs24  Specialty priests require two charges instead of the usual one charge when resurrected. The racial charge requirement remains unchanged.
\par 
\par \tab Specialty priests dedicated to deities of healing, protection, warfare, endurance, and similar areas of influence require only one charge to resurrect.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Rod of Rulership:}{\fs24  Creatures being ruled need not remain within the rod\rquote s 150\_
foot radius once they have been affected. Most creatures get no saving throw, but magic resistance and resistance to mental attack or control applies. Racial resistances to charm effects also apply.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Rod of Security:}{\fs24  This item transports creatures into a pocket dimension (see page 45) attached to the world where the rod was activated.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Rope of Entanglement:}{\fs24  Use of this item does not require an attack roll. In addition to the size limitations included in the item description, all the rope\rquote 
s targets must fit within a single area of 200 square feet or less (eight 5\_foot squares in any contiguous configuration). Targets who save vs. breath weapon can move 10 feet each round and can attack nearby creatures (but not the }{\i\fs24 
rope of entanglement}{\fs24 ).
\par \tab If other creatures fail their saving throw versus the rope, those who succeeded in their save can only move if the combined weight of those who failed is less than their maximum press. For instance, a fighter wearing a }{\i\fs24 
girdle of hill giant strength}{\fs24  could drag up to 640 lbs. of weight along with him. Of course, groups of creatures must move generally the same direction to initiate an attack.
\par \tab Entwined creatures suffer a +2 initia
tive penalty and attacks against them are at +2. Targets who fail the save are held completely immobile and cannot perform any actions that require movement; attacks against immobile creatures are made with a +4 bonus.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Spell Scrolls:}{\fs24  A scroll is a temporary magical writing that stores spell energy in a portable form; it is essentially a pre\_
cast spell waiting to be triggered. The level at which a priest spell read from scroll functions is never diminished due to planar distances (see page 49), but all scroll spells are subject to local conditions. For example, a }{\i\fs24 fireball}{\fs24 
 read from a scroll is ineffective on the Plane of Elemental Water\emdash the spell creates a harmless bubble of vapor and the writing fades. Scrolls become inert if taken to a world rated M2 (see page 47) or lower, but are not otherwise harmed.
\par \tab Spell scrolls come in two types, priest and wizard. Priests cannot use wizard scrolls and vice versa. High\_
level thieves and bards have a chance to employ either type. A character who can use spell scrolls can read any spell of the appropriate type regardless of other restrictions. For example, an illusionist, who is normally barred from casting abjuration spe
lls, can }{\i\fs24 read a dispel magic}{\fs24 
 spell from a scroll. Likewise, a priest can read priest spells from spheres normally unavailable. Note that the reader could still suffer the effects of spell failure by attempting to cast a spell that is too high a level (see }{\i\fs24 D
ungeon Master Guide}{\fs24 , Appendix 3).
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Sphere of Annihilation:}{\fs24  A wizard\rquote s maximum chance to control a }{\i\fs24 sphere of annihilation}{\fs24  without the aid of a }{\i\fs24 talisman of the sphere}{\fs24 
 is 92% for a wizard of 21st level and an 18 Intelligence/Reason. There are no additional bonuses for being higher level or having an Intelligence/Reason score grater than 18.
\par \tab A }{\i\fs24 talisman of the sphere doubles}{\fs24  a wizard\rquote s Intelligence bonus for controlling the sphere. Adjusted control scores of 100% or more indicate automatic success, but other wi
zards trying to usurp control reduce the control chance; see the }{\i\fs24 sphere of annihilation}{\fs24  }{\i\fs24 in the DMG}{\fs24  description for details.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Staff of the Magi:}{\fs24  The staff\rquote s plane travel ability is similar to the priest spell }{\i\fs24 plane shift}{\fs24 , but no forked rod is required. The staff\_
wielder must be generally familiar with the destination plane either by making a previous visit or having information about the plane from a traveler who has been there. If a character wishes to research details on an unknown plane, refer to the note at }
{\i\fs24 plane shift}{\fs24  for cost and time requirements.
\par \tab The absorption power of the staff works just like that of the }{\i\fs24 rod of absorption}{\fs24  except that the level of absorbed spell is not communicated to the staff\_wielder. The decision to absorb must be made based on 
the appearance of the magic or, in the case of invisible effects, blind luck. 
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Staff of Withering:}{\fs24  The withering effect from this item makes one of the victim\rquote s limbs shriveled and useless; it has no effect on a creature\rquote s head or body. The withering effe
ct requires three charges from the staff and must be announced at the beginning of the round. If the staff hits, roll randomly to see which limb is struck.
\par \tab If the victim is humanoid, roll 1d4 to determine which limb is struck: 1=right arm, 2=left arm, 3=right leg, and 4=left leg. A shriveled arm cannot wield a weapon or shield or be used to make unarmed attacks. The character suffers a \endash 
2 penalty to Dexterity for each shriveled arm. A humanoid cannot stand up or walk without a crutch if even one leg is shriveled. The character is reduced to a crawl and cannot make any physical attacks. The character suffers a \endash 
6 penalty to Dexterity. Getting two legs shriveled has no appreciable additional effect.
\par \tab If the target is a quadruped, the staff\_wielder can usually reach only two of the opponent\rquote s limbs. Roll 1d6 to see which one is hit: 1\endash 3=right, 4\endash 
6=left. Quadrupeds with one shriveled leg move at 2/3 their normal rate and cannot make attacks with the shriveled limb. A quadruped with two shriveled limbs cannot move or physically attack.
\par \tab Insectoid or multi\_limbed creatures should be handled like quadrupeds, but their movement is unaffected as long as they have at least two functioning limbs on each side of the body.
\par \tab Attacks on flying creatures can hit the wings. F
or example, roll 1d6 to determine which limb on a flying humanoid is struck: 1=right arm, 2=left arm, 3=right leg, 4=left leg, 5=right wing, and 6=left wing. Creatures with even one shriveled wing cannot fly.
\par \tab Limbless creatures have no appendages to be withered and suffer no ill effects from withering except damage and aging.
\par \tab If the staff\_wielder chooses to make a called shot and succeeds, do not make a random roll; the staff hits the selected area instead.
\par \tab If the critical hit rules from }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Option: Combat and Tactics}{\fs24  are in use, ignore all of the foregoing and use the hit location system from that book. The area struck suffers a \ldblquote destroyed\rdblquote 
 result if the saving throw fails, even if it is not a limb (do not roll for severity). The staff\_wielder does not need to score a critical hit to roll for hit location. If the staff\_
wielder does score a critical hit, the target suffers double damage and must roll saving throws vs. both the withering and the critical hit.
\par \tab There are several ways to repair withered limbs, but }{\i\fs24 regenerate }{\fs24 and }{\i\fs24 restoration}{\fs24 
 are the most common methods. Creatures that regenerate (through an innate ability or magical item) regain the use of a shriveled limb after regenerating the equivalent of 20 points of damage. For example, a character wearing a }{\i\fs24 
ring of regeneration}{\fs24  would recover from a shriveled limb in 20 turns, and a troll would recover in seven rounds.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Stone of Good Luck:}{\fs24  The stone\rquote s +1 (or +5%) bonus applies whenever dice are rolled to see if the character (not the character\rquote s equip
ment) avoids an adverse happening. The bonus applies to saving throws, ability checks used as saving throws, system shock rolls, resurrection survival rolls, and any other event in which chance, not skill, is the determining factor. The bonus does not app
ly to spell failure, magical item creation, learning spells, or to most proficiency checks.
\par \tab The character\rquote s good luck applies to rolls for party treasure distribution and to proficiency checks involving luck or dodging, such as gaming and tumbling.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24\cf6 Wand of Negation:}{\fs24  The wand temporary renders magical devices unable to create spell\_like effects. When a device\rquote s spell\_
like function is negated, any charges expended to produce the effect are lost, but the device is not otherwise harmed. A}{\i\fs24  wand of negation}{\fs24  has no effect on cast spells or a creature\rquote s spell\_
like abilities. The wand has an initiative modifier of +1.
\par 
\par }\pard \qc\nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\adjustright {\fs32\cf6 Chapter 4:
\par }{\fs36\cf6 Creating Magical
\par Items
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\adjustright {\fs28\cf12 Holy Water}{\fs24   The }{\i\fs24 Complete Priest\rquote s Handbook}{\fs24 
 gives rules for creating holy water. This section contains more detailed rules for high\_level campaigns.
\par \tab Any cleric or specialty priest with access to the required spells can create holy water once a week, provided a suitable font is available. The required spells are: }{\i\fs24 create water, purify food & drink*, bless*, chant,}{\fs24  and }{\i\fs24 
prayer}{\fs24 . The spells marked with an asterisk are used in reversed form to create unholy water.
\par \tab The priest must spend at least eight hours praying and meditating before casting the spells in the listed order. Any delay between finishing the casting of one spell and starting the next ruins the ceremony. The character must maintain the }{\i\fs24 
chant}{\fs24  spell for one turn. Afterward, the cleric must rest at least eight hours before casting any spells. If forced into combat during this time, the character suffers a \endash 4 penalty to all attack rolls, saving throws, and ability checks. 

\par \tab A font is a specially blessed (or cursed) basin made of precious metals contained inside an elaborate case or pedestal fitte
d with a cover. A particular font can be used only once a week, and a temple or other religious building can contain only one font. Particularly large buildings might contain one font per wing or floor, at the DM\rquote s option.
\par \tab A font\rquote s maximum capacity for creating holy water depends on its cost\emdash the more rare and expensive the font, the more favorably the deity to which it is dedicated looks upon it.
\par \tab The }{\i\fs24 create water}{\fs24  spell normally produces more water than a basin can hold; the extra water is either channeled away or assumed to be magically dissipated.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Holy Water Font Costs}{\fs24 
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx2160\tx5040\adjustright {\b\fs24 Capacity\tab Basin\tab Pedestal}{\fs24 
