\par 8 vials\tab 1d6x50+1,000 gp\tab 350 gp
\par 10 vials\tab 1d6x100+1,800 gp\tab 500 gp
\par 14 vials\tab 1d4x500+5,200 gp\tab 750 gp
\par 18 vials\tab 1d4x1,000+8,000 gp\tab 1,000 gp
\par 24 vials\tab 1d4x1,000+15,000 gp\tab 1,250 gp
\par 32 vials\tab 1d4x1,000+18,000 gp\tab 1,500 gp
\par 40 vials\tab 1d6x10,000+50,000 gp\tab 1,750 gp
\par 50 vials\tab 1d10x10,000+100,000 gp\tab 2,000 gp
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab The basin and pedestal must be specially designed and fashioned; the process requires 2d4+2 weeks.
\par \tab Holy water loses its potency if removed from the font for more than one turn unless it is placed in a specially blessed crystal or leaded glass vial. Each vial holds a quarter pint of liquid and costs 5 gp. (Copper\_
pinching characters can sell empty vials on the open market for 1d4+1 gp each.)
\par \tab A font can be defiled and made useless for creating holy water (and for }{\i\fs24 magic font}{\fs24  spells) by touching it and casting a }{\i\fs24 curse}{\fs24  spell. Fonts can be nonmagically defiled by placi
ng anything repugnant to the deity to which the font is dedicated to within its confines.
\par \tab A defiled font must be entirely remade. The font cannot be cleansed or restored, except by a }{\i\fs24 wish}{\fs24 , though the materials in the basin can be recycled and used in a new basin for 1d4+1x10% of the original cost. For example, Delsenora
\rquote s temple has a font that can hold 32 vials of holy water. The initial cost was 21,000 gp for the basin plus 1,500 gp for the pedestal. If the font is defiled, the replacement cost is 1,500 gp for the pedestal and 20\endash 
50% of the original basin cost. Delsenora rolls a 2 and must pay 30% of the original cost\emdash 6,300 gp.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 Creating Magical Items}{\fs24   Sooner or later, players in high\_level campaigns start thinking about how their characters can manufactu
re their own enchanted items. The sheer difficulty involved in item creation should be sufficient to deter characters driven by simple greed, especially if the DM follows the advice on controlling magic in Chapter 1. In a well\_
run campaign, creating magical items not only consumes more resources than it generates, it keeps the characters busy trying to find what they need to complete the process.
\par \tab Characters with a true interest in creating magical items shouldn\rquote t find the effort too costly; some things are
 more important than money or power. They may be driven by a thirst for fame or a desire to create magical items they have never found in a treasure hoard. They might even be required to create a magical item to achieve a particular goal, which creates a 
variety of adventuring possibilities as the character struggles to gather the required material components in time to complete his goal.
\par \tab This section presents a system that allows the DM to quickly determine how long creating a magical item takes, how much it costs, and how likely the attempt is to fail. This system is more detailed and complex than the one detailed in the }{\i\fs24 
Dungeon Master Guide}{\fs24 , and it is intended for high\_level campaigns in which several player characters wish to create standard magical items.
\par \tab This material is generally compatible with the rules for creating magical items presented in the }{\i\fs24 Book of Artifacts}{\fs24 ; this system is a little simpler but doesn\rquote 
t give the DM as much control over how difficult items are to make. If your players are content to create only a few standard items, this book\rquote s system should work better for you.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Requirements
\par }{\fs24 \tab To create a magical item, a character needs the appropriate level of skill, a suitable place to perform the work, the correct materials and processes to complete the item, and often the }{\i\fs24 enchant an item}{\fs24  and }{\i\fs24 
permanency}{\fs24  spells.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Character Level}{\fs24 
\par \tab Wizards can create potions and scrolls at 9th level and other items at 11th level, provided that the necessary spells are available.
\par \tab Priests can create scrolls at 7th level, potions at 9th level, and other items at 11th level.
\par \tab Warriors and rogues cannot create magical items, even if they have spellcasting ability.
\par \tab Priests and wizards can use spells on scrolls, stored in items, or cast by other characters to get the spells necessary to create magical items.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Who Can Make Which Items?}{\fs24 
\par \tab No character can make a magical book, libram, manual, tome, or artifact. Artifacts are a campaign\_shaking occurrence, reserved to the discretion of the DM. Magical writings that increase levels and ability scores are likewise unbalancing.
\par \tab Racial items, such as }{\i\fs24 boots of elvenkind}{\fs24  and }{\i\fs24 girdles of dwarvenkind}{\fs24 , can be created only by priests of the indicated race. High\_level elf priests, for example, can create }{\i\fs24 cloaks of elvenkind}{\fs24 
. If the }{\i\fs24 Exceeding Level Limits}{\fs24  optional rule (from the DMG) is not in play, only NPC demihuman clerics who have achieved the maximum level can make these items.
\par \tab Mages can make any other item if they meet the level requirements and have the necessary spells.
\par \tab Priests and specialist wizards can make only those items that they can use. A cleric, for example, cannot make a magical long sword, and a transmuter can\rquote t make a }{\i\fs24 wand of fire}{\fs24 
, which employs evocation magic. Specialist wizards, however, receive a +5% bonus to their success chances when creating items that possess abilities from their school of specialization. For example, a transmuter gets the bonus when creating a }{\i\fs24 
wand of polymorphing}{\fs24 .
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Working Space}{\fs24 
\par \tab A wizard needs a laboratory to make magical items. The laborato
ry must have at least 500 square feet of floor space (20 x 25 feet), and more is preferable. Basic furnishings and supplies cost 5,000 gp, and the character must spend an additional 500 gp a month to keep the laboratory properly equipped.
\par \tab A priest must cr
eate magical items on an altar specially dedicated to his deity. There is no basic size requirement, although a deity whose portfolio includes magic might impose one. An item to be enchanted must fit on the altar, so it benefits the priest to make the alt
ar as large and sturdy as is practical. The minimum cost for building the altar and properly consecrating it is 2,000 gp. The altar requires no special maintenance, but only the priest who performed the consecration can use the altar to enchant items
\emdash no other creature can use it while the priest lives. If the altar is defiled, the priest must consecrate it again. Before consecrating the altar, the priest must please his deity with some extraordinary service connected with the deity\rquote 
s portfolio or sphere of control. A deity of wisdom, for example, might look favorably upon a priest who writes a book of philosophy or who solves a mystery using superior judgment instead of divination spells.
\par \tab After the service is complete, the priest must pray and meditate over the altar for one week. The vigil occupies all the character\rquote s waking hours. If interrupted, the vigil must be started over again.
\par 
