\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\adjustright {\i\fs24 Footnotes:}{\fs24  }{\fs24\super 1}{\fs24  One common process also is required; }{\fs24\super 2}{\fs24 
 One exotic process also is required; }{\fs24\super 3}{\fs24  One rare and one common process also are required.
\par 
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Approval}{\fs24 
\par \tab Characters who have the required skills and equipment are not necessarily free to begin churning out magical items as they see fit. The DM must approv
e any new magical item that enters the campaign. The player should explain exactly what powers the proposed item will have. In the case of items already described in rule books, this is simply a matter of having the DM review the description and decide if
 the item is appropriate to the campaign. Once approved, the player is free to have the character begin work.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Creating the Item}{\fs24 
\par 
\par \tab Every item that is to be imbued with magic must be of the finest quality, specifically created for the purpose of placing ench
antments upon it. An item must be created from one or more special materials, each of which must undergo a certain number of special processes. A character creating a sword, for example, must first commission a rare and unusual blade to be crafted. A regu
lar sword from the local smithy cannot become a }{\i\fs24 sword +3 frost brand}{\fs24 . Creating a magical item is not a matter of picking up a few household articles and muttering an incantation.
\par \tab Table 23 lists the materials and processes various types of items require. So
me of these are more difficult to complete or acquire than others, as explained in the notes to the table. Table 23 gives the suggested number of materials and processes each item requires as well as a few suggestions. It is up to the DM to decide exactly
 what materials and processes are necessary; this requires a great deal of creativity on the DM\rquote s part. In any case, the character does not automatically know what the requirements are.
\par \tab Gathering all the necessary materials can take a lot of time. Ideally,
 the DM should create a series of adventures that allows the character to obtain everything. The character is free to get help from any other character who can be persuaded to join the hunt.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Enchanting the Item}{\fs24 
\par 
\par \tab Wizards generally begin with the }{\i\fs24 enchant an item }{\fs24 
spell to focus their magical energy and then cast additional spells to create the enchantment. If the item has a power that duplicates or closely resembles a known spell, that is the spell cast to create the enchantment. If the item has an effect th
at does not duplicate a known spell, the wizard must either research a new spell or cast some combination of spells that approximates its effects. The DM must decide which spells are necessary. See the }{\i\fs24 enchant an item }{\fs24 
spell description and the Notes to Table 23 section for more details. Most items also require a }{\i\fs24 permanency }{\fs24 spell to complete the enchantment.
\par 
\par 
\par \tab Wizards lose a point of Constitution when casting the }{\i\fs24 permanency }{\fs24 spell most magical items require. Priests do not normally suffer this loss, but the 
DM can rule that the long process that a priest must undertake is so physically taxing that it drains a point of Constitution. This loss applies only to items that would require a }{\i\fs24 permanency }{\fs24 spell if the item was created by a wizard.

\par 
\par \tab Priests do not have the }{\i\fs24 enchant an item }{\fs24 spell, and they must petition their deities to instill power into their items. The procedure is described in Chapter 10 of the }{\i\fs24 Dungeon Master Guide}{\fs24 .
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Notes to Table 23}{\fs24 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Item: }{\fs24 The type of item being created. These are divided into the same general categories as used in Appendix 3 of the }{\i\fs24 Dungeon Master Guide}{\fs24  and are further subdivided by how they can be used.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Single use:}{\fs24  Using the item once completely consumes its magic, often consuming the item itself. Examples include virtually all potions, scrolls, dusts, oils, and elixirs.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Limited use:}{\fs24  The item can be used a fixed number of times before it is consumed. Some limited\_use items can be recharged, and some have multiple functions (see the }{\b\fs24 Item Details}{\fs24 
 section). This includes most rods, staves, and wands as well as some rings and miscellaneous magical items.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Single Function:}{\fs24  The item has only one power, which usually functions continuously or on demand. Some single\_function items expend charges when used. An }{\i\fs24 amulet of proof against detection
 and location, cloak of displacement}{\fs24 , and }{\i\fs24 ring of multiple wishes}{\fs24  are all examples of this type of magic.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Multiple Function:}{\fs24  The item has more than one power. Some multiple function items are charged (and also are limited\_use items) and some are not. Noncharged items of this nature include }{\i\fs24 scarabs of protection, crystal balls
}{\fs24 , and }{\i\fs24 hammers of thunderbolts}{\fs24 .
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Material:}{\b\fs24  }{\fs24 The more powerful the item, the more unusual the material from which it is made. Materials are classified by their rarity. 
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Common:}{\fs24  The material is fairly plentiful under normal circumstances. Steel, oak staves, copper, and wool are common materials.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Rare:}{\fs24  The material is expensive and difficult to find. Silk, diamonds, roc feathers, and ebony are rare materials. Common materials gathered under unusual circumstances are also considered rare. Wood taken from a lightning\_
struck oak, wool made from fleece taken at a lamb\rquote s first shearing, and steel made in a furnace tended by a dwarven elder are rare materials.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Exotic:}{\fs24  The material is unique or unusual and cannot be purchased\emdash 
the character must undertake an adventure to obtain it. Exotic materials often exist only in a metaphorical sense. Steel smelted from the ore of a fallen star, the moon\rquote s tears, the largest scale from a great wyrm\rquote s
 tail, and a lock of a goddess\rquote s hair are exotic materials. Common or rare materials gathered in extraordinary circumstances are also considered exotic. Cloth spun from phase spider silk under the new moon, a diamond freely given from a dragon
\rquote s belly, and wood taken from a lightning\_struck treant are exotic materials.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Processes:}{\b\fs24  }{\fs24 A process is a prescribed method for accomplishing a specific task that is performed in addition to the normal steps necessary for making the item. Like materials, processes are c
lassified according to rarity. For example, making a mold to cast a ring is not a process because creating a mold is a typical step in ring\_making. However, making the ring\rquote 
s mold from a wax model fashioned from beeswax taken from a hive of giant bees is a process because it is unusual. It\rquote 
s not always easy to distinguish processes from materials, but the distinction is not important as long as the item is created using the required number of special elements.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Common:}{\fs24  The process is fairly simple and strai
ghtforward, requiring only special care or some unusual preparations. Quenching a sword in snow from a spring storm, encrusting a ring with ornamental gems, and tempering a helmet in a furnace heated with lava are common processes.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Rare:}{\fs24  The process requires extra effort or extraordinary expense. Quenching a sword\rquote s blade in snow gathered at the top of the world, honing a sword blade with a }{\i\fs24 stone of good luck}{\fs24 
, and etching an amulet with acid from a giant slug\rquote s spittle are rare processes.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Exotic:}{\fs24  The process is unique or unusual and cannot be purchased\emdash the character must undertake an adventure to complete it. Exotic processes often exist only in a metaphorical sense. Quenching a sword blade in a lover\rquote 
s sigh, heating a ring in burning ice, and bathing a shield in a knight\rquote s courage are exotic processes.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Cost:}{\b\fs24  }{\fs24 This is what the character must spend for unusual fuels and other supplies when making the item. This cost is in addition to whatever the character spends on workers\rquote  salaries, travel, professional fe
es, and purchasing the materials and processes necessary for making the item.
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Time:}{\b\fs24  }{\fs24 This is the time required to actually manufacture the item once the material components have been gathered. It does not include time spent acquiring the materials and placi
ng enchantments on the item. Time cannot be reduced by hiring extra workers, getting help from another character, or spending additional money.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Item Details}{\fs24 
\par 
\par \tab This section contains additional information about creating various types of items, including required spells and the chance to create the item.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Potions
\par }{\fs24 \tab A potion requires no spells, but the caster must first have the formula. Determining a formula from scratch requires 1d3+1 weeks and costs 100 gp a week. Time and costs are reduced to the minimum if the caster has access to }{\i\fs24 commune}
{\fs24  or }{\i\fs24 contact other plane}{\fs24  spells. If the caster has a potion to analyze or a formula from a
nother caster, the research is free and requires one week. A character must have a full dose of the potion to analyze, but the dose is not consumed in the research. The time requirement listed on Table 23 is the time the character actually spends brewing 
the potion. Once a potion\rquote s formula has been personally researched and recorded, the character need not research it again unless the record is lost.
\par \tab The chance to successfully brew a potion is 70%, +1% for every two levels of the creator, and \endash 1% for each 100 gp the potion costs (detailed on Table 23).
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Scrolls
\par }{\fs24 \tab The common material required is the paper, parchment, or papyrus as detailed in Chapter 10 of the }{\i\fs24 Dungeon Master Guide}{\fs24 . The rare material is the quill; a new quill must be used for each spell written on the scroll.
\par \tab Blending the ink is the most important process. Ink for spells of levels 1\endash 3 require a rare ingredient, spells of levels 4\endash 
6 require an exotic ingredient, and spells of levels 7+ require one rare and one exotic ingredient. Blending the ink takes one day, and the ink must be used within two weeks.
\par \tab No spells are actually cast when writing a scroll, but the character must know the relevant spell and have any required material components on hand. If normally consumed in the casting, the mater
ial components are consumed in writing the scroll. If casting the spell normally imposes a penalty on the caster, such as magical aging, creating the scroll carries the same penalty.
\par \tab The chance to successfully write a scroll is 80%, +1% for every level of the creator, and \endash 1% for each level of the written spell. There are additional adjustments for the materials used (see }{\i\fs24 DMG}{\fs24 
, Chapter 10). The DM must roll for success separately for each spell on a scroll. A scroll can hold 1d6 spells, determined secretly 
by the DM, but the character knows when the scroll is full. A failed attempt to write a spell fills the scroll but usually doesn\rquote t affect spells already written on it (see the}{\b\fs24  Failure}{\fs24  section).
\par \tab Compare protection scrolls to the level of the spell that approximates their effects to determine the success chance and type of ink required. A }{\i\fs24 protection from magic}{\fs24  scroll, for example, is similar to }{\i\fs24 antimagic shell}{
\fs24 , a 6th\_level spell.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Rings
\par }{\fs24 \tab 
Any ring requires one common process in addition to any extra materials or processes needed for multiple functions or limited use. Discovering all the steps required to make a ring requires 1d6+1 weeks of research and costs 200 gp a week. Access to }{
\i\fs24 contact other plane }{\fs24 or }{\i\fs24 commune}{\fs24  spells automatically reduces the required time to two weeks.
\par \tab A ring also requires the }{\i\fs24 enchant an item}{\fs24  and }{\i\fs24 permanency}{\fs24  spells\emdash or the equivalent priest ceremony\emdash in addition to whatever spells are needed to create the ring\rquote s powers (see page 90). Multiple\_
use rings require one spell per use, and multiple\_function rings need one set of spells for each function.
\par \tab The chance to successfully create a ring is 60%, +1% for every level of the creator, and \endash 1% for each spell and special process required (except for the }{\i\fs24 enchant an item}{\fs24 
 spell). Priests, though they do not actually cast any spells, still suffer the penalty for spells. The more complex the enchantment, the more difficult it is to successfully petition the deity to imbue the item with power.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Rods, Staves, and Wands
\par }{\fs24 \tab These items require }{\i\fs24 enchant an item}{\fs24  spells\emdash or the equivalent priest ceremonies\emdash and whatever spells are needed to create their powers. Multiple\_
function rods, staves, and wands need one spell (or set of spells) for each function. A}{\i\fs24  permanency}{\fs24  spell is required.
\par \tab The base chance to successfully create one of these items is the same as for a ring. If the item is rechargeable, it is created with one charge and then additional charges are added using the recharging procedure detailed below.
\par \tab A rod, staff, or wand loses all its magical properties if it is ever drained of all its charges, even if it is normally rechargeable. Once drained of charges, the item can never be enchanted again.
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 
\par Miscellaneous Magic, Armor, and Weapons}{\fs24 
\par \tab These items require }{\i\fs24 enchant an item}{\fs24  spells\emdash or the equivalent priest ceremonies\emdash and whatever spells are needed to create their powers. Each plus for a weapon or protective device requires a separate spell. Single\_
use and limited\_use items do not require }{\i\fs24 permanency }{\fs24 spells, but other items do.
\par \tab The chance to successfully create one of these items is the same as for a ring. Rechargeable items are created with one charge to reduce the chance for failure.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Recharging Items
\par }{\fs24 
\par \tab Any spellcaster can recharge items, provided the character has access to the required spells and the character can use the item. Mages can also recharge items usable only by fighters or rogues.
\par \tab To begin recharging, the character casts }{\i\fs24 enchant an item}{\fs24 \emdash or performs the equivalent priest ceremony\emdash to prepare the magical device and then casts spells that the item absorbs to create charges.
\par \tab The spell used to provide a charge must duplicate or nearly duplicate the item\rquote s power. If no spell duplicates the power, the character must research a new spell 
or cast some combination of spells that resembles the power. If the item has multiple functions, the spell must duplicate the item\rquote s most powerful function. For example, it takes a }{\i\fs24 cone of cold}{\fs24  spell to recharge a }{\i\fs24 
wand of frost}{\fs24 .
\par \tab Once the preparatory spell or ceremony is complete, the character has 24 hours to create charges. Each spell used requires its normal casting time (not 2d4 hours per spell level as required by the }{\i\fs24 enchant an item}{\fs24 
 spell). When the initial enchantment fades, the character can prepare the item again, but every time an item is prepared to receive charges it must save vs. spell at the caster\rquote s level with a \endash 
1 penalty. If the save fails, the item falls into useless dust.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Success and Failure
\par }{\fs24 
\par \tab Magic is tricky and involves many constantly 
changing variables, causing each enchantment to differ slightly from prior attempts. No matter how many times a character has created a particular magical item, the chances for success remain the same. A character\rquote 
s general level of expertise can improve, but particular enchantments cannot be mastered.
\par \tab Each attempt to create a magical item requires a roll for success. The DM secretly rolls the dice to see if the attempt to create a magical item succeeds. Any roll of 96\endash 00 fails automatically.
\par \tab Most failed 
attempts ruin the item, melting it into useless slag or destroying it in some other dramatic fashion. A failed attempt to write a spell on a scroll fills the remaining space with a useless blob of ink, leaving spells already successfully written intact.

\par \tab If the failure roll was a 96\endash 00, the item appears to have been created normally but has a cursed or reversed effect instead. The DM decides the nature of the curse, using the cursed items from the magical item lists in the }{\i\fs24 
Dungeon Master Guide}{\fs24  and }{\i\fs24 Tome of Magic}{\fs24  as a reference.
\par 
\par 
