\par * If the spell\rquote s range varies with the caster\rquote s level, its movement rate increases with the increased range. For example, a }{\i\fs24 fireball}{\fs24  cast by a 5th\_level wizard has a range of 60 yards (MV 4), but a }{\i\fs24 fireball }{
\fs24 cast by a 15th\_level wizard has a range of 160 yards (MV 5).
\par \tab Some spells, such as }{\i\fs24 prismatic spray}{\fs24 , have ranges listed as 0, but areas of effect that allow them to reach distant targets. These spells\rquote  movement rates are a function of their areas of effect, not their basic rang
es. Spells that allow instantaneous movement or that affect huge areas have movement rates of 5. See the Appendix for examples.
\par 
\par ** Also includes spells with a range of less than one yard.
\par 
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Type: }{\fs24 A spell\rquote s type determines what it can do and which opposing spells it must attempt to destroy. For dueling purposes, there are five types of spells:
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Attack (A):}{\fs24  The spell\rquote s normal function in the AD&D game is to harm the target in some way. In a duel, an attack spell is used to damage or temporarily incapacitate the opponent. Spells such as }{\i\fs24 charm person, web}{
\fs24 , and }{\i\fs24 disintegrate}{\fs24  are attack spells. An attack spell must conduct combat with any opposing defensive spell it meets and can conduct combat with opposing leech spells (the advantaged spellcaster decides, see be
low). It ignores missile and other attack spells.
\par \tab If an attack spell reaches the opposing spellcaster\rquote s square, it takes effect (see the }{\b\fs24 Characters vs. Spells}{\fs24  section for details).
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Defense (D):}{\fs24  The spell\rquote s normal function is to protect or fortify the recipient in some fashion. In a duel, these spells are used to destroy attack spells before they cross the arena. Spells such as }{\i\fs24 
protection from evil, cure light wounds}{\fs24 , and }{\i\fs24 minor globe of invulnerability }{\fs24 are defense spells. A defensive spell must conduct com
bat with any opposing attack spell it meets and can conduct combat with opposing leech spells. It generally ignores missiles, but some defensive spells are specifically designed to stop missiles (refer to the }{\b\fs24 
Special Dueling Characteristics for Spells}{\fs24 ) Defensive spells always ignore each other, and, upon reaching the opposing spellcaster\rquote s square, vanish without affecting the opponent.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Leech (L):}{\fs24  Outside of a duel, the spell normally does not cause harm or provide a defense. In a duel, leech spells are used to destroy other spells and to damage the opponent. Spells such as }{\i\fs24 teleport, detect invisibility}{
\fs24 , and}{\i\fs24  haste}{\fs24  are leech spells. A leech spell must conduct combat with any opposing leech spell it meets and can conduct combat with opposing attack or defense spells. It ignores missiles.
\par \tab If a leech spell reaches the opposing spellcaster\rquote s square, it inflicts 1d6 points of damage per spell level. The opponent is allowed a saving throw vs. spell to reduce the damage by half.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Attack/Defense (AD):}{\fs24  The spell\rquote s nor
mal function is to create a solid barrier or summon a creature. In a duel, an attack/defense spell creates a barrier that most spells cannot bypass without a battle and has the ability to inflict damage upon the opponent. The various wall and monster summ
oning spells as well as }{\i\fs24 dispel magic}{\fs24 
 are attack/defense spells. An attack/defense spell must conduct combat with any opposing defensive or attack spell it meets, and it can conduct combat with opposing leech or missile spells, as chosen by the advantaged spellcaster (see below).
\par \tab If an attack/defense spell reaches the opposing spellcaster\rquote s square, it usually inflicts 1d6 points of damage per spell level. The opponent is allowed a saving throw vs. spell or a spell combat roll to avoid the damage.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Missile (M):}{\fs24  The spell creates or propels a physical or magical projectile that streaks toward the target, inflicting damage. In a duel, a missile spell is used to damage the opponent. Spells such as }{\i\fs24 
fireball, magic missile,}{\fs24  and}{\i\fs24  flame arrow}{\fs24  are missile spells. A missile spell can conduct combat with opposing attack/defense spells. It ignores other missile, leech, attack, and most defense spells.
\par \tab If a missile spell reaches the opposing spellcaster\rquote s square, it takes effect (see the }{\b\fs24 Characters vs. Spells}{\fs24  section for details).
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Power Rank (PR):}{\b\fs24  }{\fs24 The higher a spell\rquote s power rank, the more likely it is to defeat another spell in combat. A spell\rquote s power rank is its level plus nine. For example, a }{\i\fs24 fireball}{\fs24  is a 3rd\_
level spell and has a PR of 12.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Movement (MV):}{\b\fs24  }{\fs24 A spell\rquote s mov
ement rate determines how quickly it moves across the arena. A spell need not move its full rate but can never exceed its movement rate in a single round. Most spells must move at least one space every round (unless locked in combat with another spell). T
he only exception is defensive spells; a defensive spell can remain in the caster\rquote 
s space instead of moving across the arena, but only one such spell can remain with the caster at any given time. If the caster leaves the space, the spell stays behind.
\par \tab A spell\rquote s movement rate is based on its range, as shown on the Table 26.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Requirements for Spellcasting}{\fs24 
\par \tab All spellcasting during a duel is simultaneous. Spells cannot be disrupted as they can during a normal encounter. Spellcasting is not always possible, however.
\par \tab To cast a spell in a duel, a character must be free from the effects of hostile spells and not engaged in personal combat with his opponent. The character must have the intended spe
ll memorized and must have any required material components in his possession. Additional components are not required, however, as the arena itself makes up for the lack. For example, the }{\i\fs24 pyrotechnics }{\fs24 
spell normally requires a fire source. In a dueling arena, the spell still works.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Spell Movement and Combat}{\fs24 
\par 
\par \tab When a spell is cast, it appears in the space immediately ahead of the caster; if it is a defensive spell, it can appear in the caster\rquote s space instead. No spell moves on the round when it is cast. If an opponent\rquote 
s spell already occupies the space, the two spells must check for combat.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Movement: }{\fs24 A spell already in the arena when a round begins moves one or more spaces toward the opponent. The spell must move at least one square forward unless it is locked or it is a defensive spell occupying the caster\rquote 
s square; the latter type of spell has a movement of 0 and remains where it is until it is destroyed in combat or the duel ends. A spell need not move its full movement rate.
\par \tab All of the advantaged caster\rquote 
s spells move first, even if combat causes the advantage to shift to the other caster. When spells move, the spell closest to the opponent always moves first, then the next closest, and so on until all the character\rquote s spells have moved.
\par \tab It is possible fo
r two or more spells to occupy the same space. All spells cast by one character can freely move through each other or stop in the same space. When two friendly spells begin a round in the same space, the spell with the fastest movement rate moves first.

\par \tab Spells from different casters also can move through each other or stop in the same space if they are not required to conduct combat. Spells never move backward unless forced to do so by an opposing spell\rquote s special ability.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Combat: }{\fs24 When two opposing spells meet, the disadvantaged caster must announce his spell\rquote s type (A, D, AD, L, or M). The advantaged caster then checks his spell\rquote 
s type against Table 25 to determine if the spells battle or if they pass each other. The advantaged caster is not required to reveal his spell\rquote 
s type, and neither caster is required to reveal what the spells actually are. Note that some spells must battle each other even if their types don\rquote t require them to (see the notes on special abilities). It is the disadvantaged opponent\rquote s re
sponsibility to announce special abilities that might be relevant.
\par \tab If combat occurs, it is conducted immediately, and all spell movement temporarily stops until the combat is resolved.
\par \tab To conduct combat between spells, each player rolls 1d20 and compares the result to his spell\rquote s power rank. If the roll is higher than the power rank, the spell fails. If the roll is equal to or less than the power rank, the spell succeeds.

\par \tab If one spell fails and the other succeeds, the failed spell is destroyed. If other 
opposing spells exist in the same space, battle is conducted with them as well. If the winning spell was moving when the combat occurred it can finish its move after the battle. It is possible for one spell to fight several different battles in a single r
ound, but combat between any one pair of spells is conducted only once each round.
\par \tab If both spells fail, the two spells lock. Failure can occur when both spells roll higher than their power scores or when two spells with the same power scores tie each other. Locked spells prevent other spells from passing; refer to }{\b\fs24 
Locked Spells}{\fs24 , below.
\par \tab If both spells succeed, the spell with the higher roll destroys the spell with the lower roll.
\par \tab For example, Rary casts a }{\i\fs24 charm person}{\fs24  spell (A, PR 10) that meets Serten\rquote s }{\i\fs24 cure light wounds}{\fs24  spell (D, PR 10). The two spells must battle. Rary rolls a 12 and Serten rolls a 1. Rary\rquote s }{\i\fs24 
charm person}{\fs24  spell is destroyed because it failed its roll and Serten\rquote s }{\i\fs24 cure light wounds }{\fs24 spell succeeds. If Rary had rolled a 9 instead of a 12, the }{\i\fs24 charm person}{\fs24  would have destroyed the }{\i\fs24 
cure light wounds}{\fs24  spell because it succeeded with a higher roll. If Serten had rolled a 20 and Rary had rolled a 12 (or if both had rolled a 10), the two spells would have locked. 
\par \tab If an opposing spell occupies the space in front of a character during step 3 and the character casts his own spell into that space, the two spells immediately check for combat.
\par 
