\par }{\fs28\cf12 True Dweomers and Quest Spells}{\fs24   As powerful as a true dweomer is, it is still mortal magic. Quest spell effects, which represent a deity\rquote 
s direct intervention in the world, generally cannot be countered by true dweomers. A dispel true dweomer is ineffective against a quest spell effect.
\par \tab If a quest spell produces a creature, object, or other effect that can be attacked or destroyed by normal means or spells, a true dweomer can be used to attack it. For example, a banish true dweomer ca
nnot remove the creatures summoned by a wolf spirits quest spell, but a slay or strike true dweomer can harm the individual }{\i\fs24 wolf spirits}{\fs24 .
\par \tab Ward true dweomers can work against quest spell effects if the protection is relevant. For example, a ward that provides protection from electrical attacks is effective against the lightning bolts generated from a }{\i\fs24 storm of vengeance }{
\fs24 quest spell, but not the spell\rquote s other effects.
\par \tab If the quest spell allows a saving throw, the ward works normally. If the quest spell does 
not allow a saving throw, anything protected by a ward gains a saving throw of 18. If the ward provides magic resistance, its value is halved vs. quest spell effects.
\par 
