\par }{\fs32\cf6 Chapter 7:
\par }{\fs36\cf6 High\_Level 
\par Characters
\par }{\fs24 Chapter 1 explained that high\_level characters are unusual and heroic. This chapter includes rules for giving high\_
level characters abilities that allow them to function as epic heroes that are truly different from lesser mortals. It also explains some limitations that even epic heroes must face.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 Saving Throws}{\fs24   All characters have the ability to resist hostile magi
c by rolling saving throws. This ability is quite potent at high levels. Chapter 1 discusses some of the problems this can cause in the campaign. This section includes rules to help ease some of those problems.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 A Reminder: }{\fs24 Every character group has a leve
l beyond which saving throws cannot be further improved. Priests reach this limit at level 19, rogues reach it at level 21, warriors reach it at level 17, and wizards reach it at level 21. See Table 60 in the }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Handbook}{\fs24 
 for details.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Automatic Saving Throw Failure}{\fs24 
\par 
\par \tab Barring some special circumstance that makes a saving throw unnecessary, such as a successful magic resistance roll or immunity to a particular attack form, there is always a chance that a character can fail a saving throw.
 All characters and most other creatures fail their saving throws on rolls of 3 or less on 1d20, no matter how many bonuses they receive to the roll from magical items, spells, ability scores, and the like.
\par \tab Some beings have lower failure numbers: Lesser deities fail their saving throws on rolls of 2 or less, intermediate deities fail on rolls of 1, and greater deities need not roll at all\emdash they never fail their saving throws.
