\par }{\fs28\cf12 Skills for High-Level Characters}{\fs24   Characters who become sufficiently advanced in their professions begin to develop bags of tricks that less\_accomplished characters can\rquote 
t match. These special abilities are similar to nonweapon proficiencies, but characters cannot learn skills from outside their groups. Each skill has a much more dramatic effect than a proficiency and has a minimum level requirement associated with it.

\par \tab Just like proficiencies, many skills require a die roll to determine if they work. Success is determined by rolling the number indicated or less on 1d20. In most cases, a character\rquote 
s ability scores can alter the chance for success; these adjustments are listed on Table 41. A skill\rquote s requirements, success numbers, and relevant abilities are listed after each skill description and are compiled in Table 40 for quick reference.

\par \tab A few skills can be used a limited number of times each day. The success numbers for these skills drop by a fixed amount each time they are used. Once a skill\rquote s base score 
is reduced to zero or less, the character cannot use that skill for a set period of time, usually one day. Any other skill based upon the reduced skill is likewise unavailable. Even if the character\rquote 
s ability adjustment (from Table 41) raises the skill score above zero, the skill remains unavailable until the indicated time has passed. The skill\rquote s base score returns to normal after the listed time has elapsed.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Opposed Success Rolls: }{\fs24 In some cases, a skill requires an opposed roll in which the two creatures
 involved both roll 1d20 against an ability score or skill success number. If one opponent fails the roll, the creature who succeeded wins the contest. If both make their rolls, the opponent with the higher roll wins. If both opponents fail, some unusual 
result usually occurs. All ties are re\_rolled.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Acquiring and Improving Skills: }{\fs24 Characters who meet a skill\rquote s level requirement can learn the skill by spending proficiency slots. If the character point system from the }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Option: Skills & Powers }{
\fs24 book is in play, the character can spend points instead.
\par \tab Each skill\rquote s cost is listed after the skill description. The first number is the cost in nonweapon proficiency slots unless otherwise indicated. The number in parentheses is the skill\rquote s character point cost.
\par \tab A skill\rquote s base success number can be improved by devoting extra slots or character points to the skill (see the }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Handbook}{\fs24 , Chapter 5, and }{\i\fs24 Skills & Powers}{\fs24 
, Chapter 6). No mortal can ever have a base score of more than 16 in any skill; }{\i\fs24 wishes }{\fs24 have no effect on this limit.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf12 Warriors}{\fs24   High\_level warriors are formidable killing machines, especially when equipped with magical weapons and armor. Although their THAC0 ceases to improve once they reach level 20, they continue to receive extra proficienci
es and hit points until level 30. In addition, warriors automatically receive special powers beginning at 21st level. These abilities vary according to the warrior\rquote 
s subclass. All warriors also have access to a host of special skills beginning at level 10.
\par 
