\par }{\fs28\cf6 Fighters Beyond 20th Level
\par }{\fs24 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Breech Immunity: }{\fs24 Beginning at 21st level, a fighter using any weapon\emdash including his bare hands\emdash can harm creatures that are no
rmally hit only by +1 or better magical weapons. The fighter does not actually get an attack or damage bonus but can harm creatures such as a lycanthropes with any physical attack. This power is not magical and is not diminished by factors such as planar 
distances or effects that disrupt magic.
\par \tab At 24th level, a fighter can harm creatures that are hit only by +2 or better weapons. This ability increases to allow the fighter to strike creatures that require a +3 weapon at 27th level, and by 30th level, the w
arrior can strike creatures that require +4 weapons.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Intimidation:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 A fighter of 21st level or higher has the ability to shake an enemy\rquote s resolve before combat begins. To use this ability, the fighter must be in plain sight and close enough to see the opponent\rquote 
s face clearly (10 yards if the visibility is good). Use of this ability does not constitute an attack, nor is there any initiative modifier. 
\par \tab Opponents with 4+1 Hit Dice/levels or less automatically retreat from the confrontation. The creature does 
not flee in panic, but cautiously backs away. Intimidated creatures seek to avoid any confrontation with the fighter for the rest of the day. If the fighter or his party attacks, the creature is free to return the attack, suffering the penalties as detail
ed below.
\par \tab If the opponent has more than 4+2 hit dice/levels, it is entitled to a saving throw vs. death magic to escape the effects. If the saving throw is failed, the creature can opt to retreat from the fighter or remain in the area, in which case it suf
fers a \endash 
2 penalty on all initiative, attack, saving throw, and ability check rolls as long as the fighter remains within 60 feet of the creature. The creature is free to leave the area to avoid the effects, launching missile attacks or directing other acti
vities, but the penalties return once the creature gets within 60 feet of the fighter.
\par \tab As a fighter increases in level, he becomes increasingly more intimidating. For every three levels that the fighter gains after receiving this ability, the opponent saves at an additional \endash 
2 penalty. Thus, after achieving 24th level, opponents save at \endash 2, at 27th level }{\fs24\cf1 the penalty}{\fs24  increases to \endash 4, and a 30th\_level fighter inflicts a \endash 6 saving throw adjustment.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Rangers Beyond 20th Level
\par }{\fs24 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Extra Followers: }{\fs24 At 21st level, a ranger attracts 2d6 more followers. The ranger attracts another group of 2d6 followers at 26th level.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Scroll and Magical Item Use: }{\fs24 At 21st level, a ranger can read priest scrolls. The ranger\rquote s level is considered to be 9th when determining the chance of spell failure. The ranger can also use priest magical items at this level.

\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Scroll Writing: }{\fs24 At 24th level, a ranger can prepare scrolls of priest spells according to the rules in Chapter 4. The ranger\rquote s level for purposes of determining success or failure is considered to be 9th.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Scrying: }{\fs24 At 27th level, a ranger can employ }{\fs24\cf6 crystal balls }{\fs24 and other scrying devices as a 9th\_level wizard.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Paladins Beyond 20th Level
\par }{\fs24 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Scroll and Magical Item Use: }{\fs24 At 21st level, a paladin can read priest scrolls. The paladin\rquote 
s level is considered to be 9th when determining the chance of spell failure. The paladin can also use priest magical items at this level.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Scroll Writing: }{\fs24 At 24th level, a paladin can prepare scrolls of priest spells according to the rules in Chapter 4. The paladin\rquote s level for purposes of determining success or failure is considered to be 9th.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Disease Immunity: }{\fs24 At 27th level, a paladin becomes immune to all forms of disease, even cursed afflictio
ns, such as mummy rot and lycanthropy, but not disease inflicted by true dweomers (see Chapter 6) or the special powers of priests that are higher level than the paladin. At this level, the paladin\rquote s }{\i\fs24 cure disease }{\fs24 
ability is potent enough to cure cursed afflictions, such as mummy rot and lycanthropy, and remove any form of curse or affliction cast by a character of lower level than the paladin. This applies to cursed magical items as well.
\par 
\par }{\fs28\cf6 Skills For High-Level Warriors}{\fs24 
\par 
\par \tab The following skills are available to fighters, rangers, and paladins who meet the listed requirements.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Adaptation: }{\fs24 A character with this skill has a trained mind that quickly analyzes unusual or unfavorable environments and a finely tuned body that can compensate for physical impediments to fighting.
\par \tab Warriors who use this skill successfully do not suffer combat and initiative penalties for fighting in an unfavorable environment, most notably the +6 foreign environment penalty to initiative (see Chapter 9 of the }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Handbook}
{\fs24 ). If the 
environment also includes special saving throws or ability checks due to physical conditions, such as a Dexterity/Balance check to avoid falling off a ladder when struck in melee, characters successfully using this skill receive a +3 (or +15%) bonus to th
e check.
\par \tab The skill does not allow characters to ignore situational movement penalties, environmental factors that are not combat related, or conditions that are physically impossible to overcome. For example, no one can avoid sinking into quicksand without
 magical aid, and resistance from water still makes slashing and bludgeoning weapons almost useless without a }{\i\fs24 ring of free action }{\fs24 or a }{\i\fs24 free action }{\fs24 
spell. Likewise, characters adapted to fighting underwater still have to find ways to breathe.
\par \tab The skill has no effect on penalties derived from an environment\rquote s magical properties or on penalties based on vision or lighting. In fact, characters who cannot observe their surroundings suffer a \endash 4 penalty to the skill check\emdash 
it is very hard to size up battlefield in thick fog or pitch darkness.
\par \tab To use this skill, a character must actually enter combat in a foreign or unfavorable environment. At the end of each round, the character checks the skill. The skill check requires only a moment\rquote s thought and can be checked 
every round until successful. It does not prevent other actions\emdash melee attacks, spellcasting, etc.\emdash during the round. Once the skill succeeds, the character temporarily ignores the environment\rquote 
s special effects, as described above. The effect lasts for the e
ntire battle plus one day per character level thereafter, and this can be extended indefinitely if the character practices fighting in the environment for at least eight hours a week. At an extra cost of one weapon or nonweapon proficiency slot (or three 
character points), the character can become permanently adjusted to fighting in the environment, provided the additional cost is paid before the adaptation fades.
\par 
