\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Captivate:}{\b\fs24  }{\fs24 Warriors with this skill have developed a strong personal presence that is attractive to members of their own races.
\par \tab Even without a skill roll, children, members of the opposite sex, and other warriors tend to be friendly toward the character provided they are of the same race an
d their alignments are similar to that of the character. For purposes of this skill, alignments are similar when they share one common element: law, neutrality, chaos, good, or evil.
\par \tab Friendly nonplayer characters tend to pay attention to the warrior and v
iew what the warrior says or does in a favorable light as long as it is not obviously harmful or contrary to local customs. With a minimum of encouragement, a friendly NPC gives the warrior information, performs simple errands, makes introductions, and so
 on. The warrior is a celebrity in the friendly character\rquote s eyes.
\par \tab The warrior can attempt a skill roll to make a suggestion (as the 3rd\_level wizard spell) to a friendly NPC if the warrior can speak to the person privately for a few minutes. The NPC automa
tically obeys the suggestion if it does not involve risk, loss, or potential embarrassment; otherwise, a save vs. spell applies. If the warrior gives the NPC an appropriate gift of modest value or does a useful favor, the saving throw is made at a 
\endash 4 penalty. If the saving throw succeeds, the NPC tries to flee from the warrior\rquote s presence. Each attempt to plant a }{\i\fs24 suggestion }{\fs24 reduces the warrior\rquote 
s base bravery score by two. Once the base bravery score falls to zero, the warrior\rquote s captivate skill is lost for the day.
\par \tab Creatures with 8 or more Hit Dice/levels are immune to this skill\rquote s effects. Any hostile act or threat by the warrior\emdash including a failed suggestion\emdash breaks this skill\rquote s effects. 
\par \tab If the warrior spends an extended period of time (a few hours or more
) in personal contact with a single NPC who is susceptible to this skill, the DM can secretly roll a skill check. If the check succeeds, the NPC forms an attachment to the character and acts upon the attachment in some way. More often than not, these atta
chments prove to  be troublesome to the character. A few suggestions are listed below:
\par 
\par   Stows away in the PC\rquote s baggage.
\par   Offers to serve the warrior.
\par   Brags about relationship with hero.
\par   Undertakes a dangerous task on the PC\rquote s behalf.
\par   Unfavorably compares a powerful local figure\emdash perhaps a deity\emdash with the character.
\par   Steals a trinket or minor item from the hero.
\par   Seeks marriage with (or adoption by) the PC.
\par   Claims to be the warrior\rquote s spouse or offspring.
\par   Presents PC with an apparently useless gift.
\par 
