\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Hardiness: }{\fs24 
Warriors with this skill can use their inner strength to temporarily delay the harmful effects of special attacks, but not physical damage. If given sufficient time to rest, this skill allows warriors to recover from such attacks without additional aid.

\par \tab When subjected to a harmful special effect from a spell, creature, or magical item, a warrior can roll a skill check. If the check succeeds, the effect is delayed for the period of time shown on the table below. If the check fails, the war
rior suffers the effect normally. If the effect allows a saving throw, the warrior rolls the save before checking this skill.
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\adjustright {\b\fs24 Warrior Level\tab Delay}{\fs24 
\par 15\endash 19\tab 5 Rounds
\par 20\endash 24\tab 10 Rounds
\par 25\endash 29\tab 15 Rounds
\par 30\tab 20 Rounds
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab Hardiness does not delay simple damage from any source, including continuing damage, such as }{\i\fs24 Melf\rquote s acid arrow }{\fs24 or a }{\i\fs24 sword of wounding}{\fs24 
, or special forms of purely physical damage, such as severed limbs or broken bones. Nor does this skill protect against effects that are not directly harmful, such as magical charms, entanglement, or imprisonment.
\par \tab If the skill succeeds, the warrior suffers no harm from the effect until the delay ends, but he is aware of what the effect is. If the warrior receives the appropriate cure before the delay ends, there is no ha
rm to the character. If the harmful effect\rquote s duration is shorter than the delay there also is no effect.
\par \tab For example, a 17}{\fs24\super th}{\fs24 -level fighter with 34 hit points left (from a total of 115) is battling a wizard who casts power word kill on him. After making hi
s hardiness skill roll, he delays the effects of the spell for five rounds. Two rounds later, after killing the wizard, the party priest casts heal on him. When the delay effect is over, the power word kill effect occurs, but his hit points are now over 6
0 and he is immune to the spell. If the priest had not healed him, he could have lapsed into unconsciousness in an effort to avoid the effect of the power word, as detailed below.
\par \tab If the harmful effect has not expired or been cured when the delay ends, the warrior must either suffer the full force of the effect or lapse into unconsciousness while the character\rquote 
s body struggles to resist. The length of time the character remains unconsciousness depends on the extent of the injury, as noted on the table below.
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\adjustright {\b\fs24 Injury\tab Recovery Time
\par }{\fs24 Minor\tab 1 Day
\par Severe\tab 1 Week
\par Extreme\tab 1 Month
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\adjustright {\fs24 
\par }{\i\fs24 Minor: }{\fs24 These effects impair\emdash but do not incapacitate\emdash the character, such as blindness, deafness, and ability score reductions. Spells such as }{\i\fs24 antipathy/sympathy }{\fs24 (antipathy effect), }{\i\fs24 cause bli
ndness/deafness, color spray }{\fs24 (most of the time), }{\i\fs24 contagion}{\fs24 , and }{\i\fs24 holy word }{\fs24 are examples of minor effects.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24 Severe: }{\fs24 Includes effects that incapacitate or completely transform the character, such as petrification, polymorphing, and death. Severe spell effects include }{\i\fs24 hold person, phantasmal killer, polymorph other, power word kill}{
\fs24 , and }{\i\fs24 power word stun}{\fs24 .
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24 Extreme: }{\fs24 Includes effects that wrench the character\rquote s very being, such as energy drains (per level drained) and magical aging (per year aged). Two notable examples include the 9}{\fs24\super th}{\fs24 -level wizard spell }{
\i\fs24 energy drain }{\fs24 and the 6th\_level priest spell age creature from the }{\i\fs24 Tome of Magic}{\fs24 .
\par 
\par \tab At the end of the recovery time, the character attempts a system shock roll. If the roll succeeds, the character awakens\emdash fully healed\emdash 
and the effects of the delayed magic are nullified. If the roll fails, the character awakens, but suffers the full force of the effect. Thus, it is 
possible for a character to rest for months and then wake up only to die from the effects of the delayed magic.
\par \tab For instance, if our fighter from the above example had decided to sleep off the effects of the }{\i\fs24 power word kill}{\fs24 , he would have been unconscious for a week. The player rolls the character\rquote 
s system shock roll (17 Constitution) and rolls 98%\emdash 1% over what he needed. Since the power word would have killed him at the time he lapsed into unconsciousness, the character awakens just long enough to be killed by the spell.
\par \tab If a character is suffering from multiple effects, the character makes system shock rolls at the end of each recovery period. The player can choose the order in which the checks are made, but the character does not awaken until checks hav
e been made for all the effects. For example, a character who was drained by a vampire and paralyzed by a lich remains unconscious for nine weeks\emdash 
one month to revive from each of the two levels the vampire drained and another week for the paralysis.
\par \tab If an appropriate cure is applied while the character is unconscious, the character recovers immediately and automatically. For instance, the fighter sleeping off the effects of the }{\i\fs24 power word kill }{\fs24 
spell from the previous example would recover after receiving healing sufficient to raise his hit points above 60.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Heroic Effort: }{\fs24 As a last\_ditch effort to continue a battle, a fighter can use the hardiness skill to extend his life. Instead of dying at zero hit points (or falling unconscious at zero hit points and dying at \endash 
10 if the optional }{\i\fs24 Hovering at Death\rquote s Door }{\fs24 rule is in play), a warrior who makes a successful hardiness check can continue fighting until reaching \endash 
20 hit points. The character can function in this state for a number of rounds equal to his delay effect, suffering the appropriate consequences for reduced hit points at the end of the delay.
\par \tab Use of this skill is not without its drawbacks, however. If the warrior is reduced to \endash 20 hit points or less, the character is struck unconscious and dies once the delay expires. Once a character\rquote s hit points drop to \endash 
20 or less, death is inevitable at the end of the delay period unless unusual conditions exist (see below).
\par \tab Curative spells can allow a warrior struck unconscious by being reduced \endash 20 hit points or less to rejoin a battle, but death still occurs at the end of the delay period. A }{\i\fs24 raise dead }{\fs24 or }{\i\fs24 resurrection }{\fs24 
spell cast during the delay period prevents death from occurring.
\par \tab It is possible that a character with the ability to regenerate (from a magical item or high Constitution score) can continue fighting even after being reduced to \endash 20 hit points or less. If regeneration increases the warrior\rquote 
s hit points to \endash 19 or more during the delay period, the character regains consciousness and does not die if he 
receives enough healing to restore him to positive hit points before the delay ends. Even if death occurs, regeneration usually restores a character much more quickly than the rest period the hardiness skill requires. A character returned to life through 
regeneration need not make a system shock roll.
\par \tab An }{\i\fs24 amulet of life protection }{\fs24 also allows a warrior to continue fighting after being reduced to \endash 20 hit points or less if the character receives sufficient healing to restore him to \endash 19 hit points or more. The
 character still lapses back into death at the end of the delay period, but any form of magical healing can restore him to life (see page 79) and no system shock roll is required.
\par \tab Each use of the hardiness skill lowers the warrior\rquote s base bravery score by two for the rest of the day. The skill is ineffective if the character\rquote s base bravery score has been reduced to zero or less.
\par 
