\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Sage Ability: }{\fs24 At 21st level, a wizard has accumulated enough books and esoteric knowledge to function as a sage (see }{\i\fs24 Dungeon Master Guide}{\fs24 
, Chapter 12). The wizard can answer general questions in two categories listed on table 61 in the }{\i\fs24 Dungeon Master Guide}{\fs24 , but one category must be Alchemy or Chemistry. This reflects the character\rquote 
s knowledge of magical potions and scroll inks. The player can choose the remaining category or the DM can assign one.
\par \tab The character\rquote s base chance to answer a general question is 6 or less on 1d20, modified by the character\rquote s Intelligence/Knowledge bonus.
\par \tab At 24th level, the character can answer specific questions after acquiring 1d6 books or scrolls at a cost of 1,000 gp each.
\par \tab At 27th level, the character can answer exacting questions after acquiring 1d6 additional books or scrolls at a cost of 1,000 gp each.
\par \tab It is always possible for a chara
cter to discover the answer to a difficult question by consulting another character, undertaking an adventure the DM has devised, or by engaging in very lengthy and expensive research. See the magical item creation rules in Chapter 4 of this book and the 
spell research rules in Chapter 7 of the }{\i\fs24 Dungeon Master Guide }{\fs24 for guidelines. 
\par \tab For example, a high\_level wizard with a knowledge of history might discover the answer to a fairly simple, but specific, question (What was the name of King Rassmon\rquote s oldest dau
ghter?) after 1d3+1 weeks of study at a cost of 100 gp a week. This is the same as the cost to discover a potion formula. A very difficult and esoteric question (Who made the carpet that lay in king Rassmon\rquote 
s great hall?) might require as much time and money to research as a 6th\_ or 7th\_level spell. The chance to know the answer would be the same as that for successfully researching the spell.
