\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Spell Sculpting:}{\b\fs24  }{\fs24 A wizard with this skill knows one spell so well the character can alter its statistics when casting it.
\par \tab To use this skill, the wizard must prepare an item to help focus and reshape the spell\rquote s magical energy. A focus item can be a small object such as a jewel, wand, or amulet. A focus item must be worth at least 500 gp per spell level.
\par \tab When sculpting, the wizard must meet all the usual requirements 
to cast the spell; the wizard employs the focus item as an additional material component, which is normally not consumed. A successful skill roll allows the wizard to change the spell in a minor way, as listed below. If the roll fails, the spell is cast n
ormally. If failure roll is a 20, the focus item is destroyed.
\par \tab When a wizard successfully sculpts a spell, the character can choose one effect from the list below.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Increase Damage: }{\fs24 The spell can inflict an extra two dice of damage of the type normally rolled to determine the damage the spell inflicts. For example, Neja, a 20th\_level wizard, could sculpt a }{\i\fs24 burning hands }{\fs24 
spell to inflict 3d3+20 points of damage. If Neja sculpted a }{\i\fs24 fireball }{\fs24 spell to increase damage, the spells would inflict 12d6 points of damage.
\par \tab Spells that have individual damage dice, such as magic missile, are not increased individually. For example, Neja could cast a }{\i\fs24 magic missile }{\fs24 
that inflicted a total of 7d4+7 points of damage. The additional damage could be added to a single missile or an additional die of damage could be added to two separate missiles.
\par \tab Spells that do not use dice rolls to determine damage cannot be sculpted in this manner.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Extend Duration:}{\fs24  The spell\rquote s total duration is doubled. This applies only to the actual time a spell lasts but not to any special durations a spell may have.
\par \tab For instance, }{\i\fs24 invisibility }{\fs24 would last a total of 48 hours or until the creature made an attack. Neja\rquote s }{\i\fs24 stoneskin }{\fs24 
would have a duration of 48 hours or until it had blocked 1d4+10 attacks (refer to the spell commentary for }{\i\fs24 stoneskin}{\fs24 ). Spells with instantaneous durations cannot be sculpted in this manner.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Extend Range: }{\fs24 The spell\rquote s range is doubled. If the spell has a range of touch or 0, its range cannot be extended.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Shorten Casting Time:}{\fs24  The spell\rquote s casting time is cut in half, to a minimum of 1. If local conditions lengthen the spell\rquote s casting time (see page 47), apply the multiplier first, then cut the result in half.
\par \tab It is not possible to sculpt a spell more than once. It is possible, however, to employ a metamagic spell, such as }{\i\fs24 extension }{\fs24 or }{\i\fs24 squaring the circle}{\fs24 
, to a spell that has been sculpted provided that the sculpting effect does not duplicate the metamagic spell effect. For example, a wizard cannot extend a spell\rquote s duration and use an }{\i\fs24 extension }{\fs24 
spell at the same time. A wizard could sculpt a spell to extend its range and use and }{\i\fs24 extension }{\fs24 spell to increase its duration.
