\par \tab At the end of the recovery time, the character attempts a system shock roll. If the roll succeeds, the character awakens\emdash fully healed\emdash and the effects of the delayed magic are nullified. If th
e roll fails, the character awakens, but suffers the full force of the effect. Thus, it is possible for a character to rest for months and then wake up only to die from the effects of the delayed magic.
\par \tab For instance, if our priest from the above example had decided to sleep off the effects of the }{\i\fs24 power word kill}{\fs24 , he would have been unconscious for a week. The player rolls the character\rquote 
s system shock roll (15 Constitution) and rolls 96%\emdash 6% over what he needed. Since the power word would have killed him at the time he lapsed into unconsciousness, the character awakens just long enough to be killed by the spell.
\par \tab If a character is suffering from multiple effects, the character makes system shock rolls at the end of each recovery period. The player can choose 
the order in which the checks are made, but the character does not awaken until checks have been made for all the effects. For example, a character who was drained by a vampire and paralyzed by a lich remains unconscious for nine weeks\emdash 
one month to revive from each of the two levels the vampire drained and another week for the paralysis.
\par \tab If an appropriate cure is applied while the character is unconscious, the character recovers immediately and automatically. 
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Heroic Effort: }{\fs24 As a last\_ditch effort to continue a battle, a priest can use the invincibility skill to extend his life. Instead of dying at zero hit points (or falling unconscious at zero hit points and dying at \endash 
10 if the optional }{\i\fs24 Hovering at Death\rquote s Door }{\fs24 rule is in play), a priest who makes a successful invincibility check can continue fighting until reaching \endash 
20 hit points. The character can function in this state for a number of rounds equal to his delay effect, suffering the appropriate consequences for reduced hit points at the end of the delay.
\par \tab Use of this skill is not without its drawbacks, however. If the priest is reduced to \endash 20 hit points or less, the character is struck unconscious and dies once the delay expires. Once a character\rquote s hit points drop to \endash 
20 or less, death is inevitable at the end of the delay period unless unusual conditions exist (see below).
\par \tab Curative spells can allow a priest struck unconscious by being reduced \endash 20 hit points or less to rejoin a battle, but death still occurs at the end of the delay period. A }{\i\fs24 raise dead }{\fs24 or }{\i\fs24 resurrection }{\fs24 
spell cast during the delay period prevents death from occurring.
\par \tab It is possible that a character with the ability to regenerate (from a magical item or high Constitution score) can continue fighting even after being reduced to \endash 20 hit points or less. If regeneration increases the priest\rquote 
s hit points to \endash 19 or more during the delay period, the character regains consciousness and does not die if he receives enough healing to restore him to positive hit points before the delay ends. Even if de
ath occurs, regeneration usually restores a character much more quickly than the rest period the invincibility skill requires. A character returned to life through regeneration need not make a system shock roll.
\par \tab An }{\i\fs24 amulet of life protection }{\fs24 also allows a priest to continue fighting after being reduced to \endash 20 hit points or less if the character receives sufficient healing to restore him to \endash 
19 hit points or more. The character still lapses back into death at the end of the delay period, but any form of magical healing can restore him to life (see page 79) and no system shock roll is required.
\par \tab Each use of the invincibility skill lowers the priest\rquote s base eminence score by two for the rest of the day. The skill is ineffective if the character\rquote s base eminence score is reduced to zero or less.
