\par \tab The priest has no control over the precise effect of the smiting, which the DM chooses from the list below. No matter what the effect, only the priest\rquote 
s enemies are harmed. Allies, innocent bystanders, and worshippers in good standing with the priest\rquote s deity are not affected.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Awestruck: }{\fs24 This is the default effect when the deity does not feel the targets are offensive or threatening. It also is granted when the deity feels that a simple demonstration of its power is sufficient to handle the situation.

\par \tab A two\_dimensional depiction of the deity or the deity\rquote s symbol appears in front of the priest, causing the priest\rquote 
s enemies to stare at it in awe if they are within the cone. Creatures with more than 10 Hit Dice or levels are allowed a saving throw vs. spell. Even if the save s
ucceeds, creatures are awestruck for one round, regardless of personal immunities granted by spell or high ability scores.
\par \tab Creatures remain awestruck only as long as the priest remains motionless and concentrates on the effect, but no longer than one roun
d per level of the priest, plus 1d8 rounds, in any case. Enemies who enter the cone after the image appears are fully subject to the awe effect, even if they avert their eyes. Any motion or the slightest break in the priest\rquote 
s concentration ends the effect, as does any offensive action by the priest\rquote s allies. Distractions to the awestruck characters also end the effect. Loud noises, magical displays, attacks, or attempts to steal the awestruck creatures\rquote 
 equipment, damage their property, or otherwise cause them harm are sufficient to break the effect.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Blinded: }{\fs24 Deities grant this effect when they find the target creatures offensive, but not particularly dangerous.
\par \tab Only creatures with 12 or more levels or Hit Dice are allowed a saving throw vs. breath weapon to avoid the effect.
\par \tab Blinded creatures move at 2/3 the normal rate and suffer a \endash 4 penalty to attack rolls, saving throws, initiative ro
lls, and ability checks. Blinded spellcasters cannot cast spells unless they can touch their targets. Blindness lasts until the victim receives a }{\i\fs24 heal }{\fs24 or }{\i\fs24 cure blindness or deafness }{\fs24 spell.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Burned: }{\fs24 This effect is granted when the targets are violent and pos
e an immediate physical threat to the priest, a sacred site, or to creatures friendly toward the priest. The cone fills with a mass of heatless flames that burn the priest\rquote 
s enemies for 6d8 points of damage, save vs. breath weapon for half. Creatures killed by the flames are reduced to piles of ash and no regeneration is possible. Though the heatless flames do not harm the area, they can affect the target\rquote 
s equipment. If a target creature fails the breath weapon saving throw or is killed by the flames, any equipment the creature carries must save vs. disintegration or be reduced to dust.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Deafened: }{\fs24 This effect occurs when the targets have not greatly offended the deity or when the priest is not in significant peril. Only creatures with 12 or more levels or H
it Dice are allowed a saving throw vs. breath weapon to avoid the effect.
\par \tab Deafened creatures move at 3/4 the normal rate and suffer a \endash 2 penalty to attack rolls, initiative rolls, and ability checks. There is a 50% chance that any spell a deafened spellca
ster attempts fails outright. Deafness lasts until the victim receives a }{\i\fs24 heal }{\fs24 or }{\i\fs24 cure blindness or deafness }{\fs24 spell.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Death: }{\fs24 Deities grant this effect only when deeply offended or when the priest is in extreme danger. Enemies within the cone are immediately 
reduced to dust if they have 4 Hit Dice/levels or less. Other enemies suffer a doubled burning effect (12d8 points of damage, save vs. spells for half damage).
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Immobilized: }{\fs24 Deities commonly grant this effect when the targets are genuinely dangerous or off
ensive. Creatures of 6+1 Hit Dice/levels or less are automatically paralyzed, their flesh turning into a rigid, crystalline substance that is easily shattered. Other creatures are allowed a save vs. petrification to negate the effect. The immobility lasts
 1d4+2 turns. }{\i\fs24 Free action }{\fs24 is useless against this effect.
\par \tab Immobilized creatures can be killed by striking their crystalline bodies with any hard object. For each strike, the creature must make a saving throw as rock crystal versus crushing blow.
 Only bonuses granted from magical rings, cloaks, and armor count toward the saving throw. Failure results in death.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Plagued: }{\fs24 The deity grants this effect when the targets are offensive, but do not pose any danger to the deity\rquote s interests. The priest\rquote s enemies are affected as if struck by a }{\i\fs24 cause disease }{\fs24 
spell. The disease inflicted is debilitating (see the reversed form of the 3rd\_level priest spell }{\i\fs24 cure disease}{\fs24 ) and causes the victim to radiate a foul stench. The disease is neither contagious nor fatal. It can be cured by a }{\i\fs24 
cure disease }{\fs24 or }{\i\fs24 breath of life }{\fs24 spell from a caster of higher level that the priest who called down the plague.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Slowed: }{\fs24 This effect is granted when deity is offended, but the enemies pose no immediate threat to a sacred site, the pries
t, or creatures friendly toward the priest or the deity. The effect is the same as the 3rd level wizard spell }{\i\fs24 slow}{\fs24 , except that creatures with less than 8+1 Hit Dice or levels get no saving throw. The slow effect lasts for 1d4+2 turns.

\par \tab Each use of this ability reduces the base score for the priest\rquote s eminence skill by two for one week. As long the priest has a positive eminence score, the priest need not make a skill roll to smite enemies. If the priest\rquote 
s base eminence skill score is zero or less, however, 
the priest must roll an eminence check to successfully smite enemies. Note that this is possible only if the priest has a Charisma/Leadership bonus large enough to offset a negative base score. If the roll fails, the deity is displeased. At best, no smite
 effect occurs, at worst, the deity directs a smite effect upon the priest.
\par \tab An example: Marissa has an eminence score of 4 and a Charisma/Leadership bonus of +4. She can freely smite enemies twice a week as long as her eminence base score is not reduced by
 using other skills. During an adventure, she uses her smite skill once and the divine voice skill once. This reduces her eminence skill to zero. If she wishes to use her smite skill again, she must roll a 4 or less on 1d20 to succeed.
\par \tab Undaunted, Marissa smites a group of passing kobolds she has come upon. Her 1d20 roll is a 3, so the smite works. Marissa\rquote s deity isn\rquote 
t too worried about the kobolds, who were minding their own business when Marissa came along, and grants Marissa an awe effect. The kobolds are briefly entranced, but a fighter from Marissa\rquote s party wades into the kobolds, making an all\_
round attack. The attack breaks the awe effect.
\par \tab Feeling threatened by the swarm of kobolds, Marissa foolishly tries to smite them again. Her base eminence score is now a \endash 
2, but her Charisma/Leadership bonus increases that to a 2. Melissa rolls an 18 and fails. The deity is getting a little tired of all Melissa\rquote s attempts to call for special aid and bestows a deafness effect on her. Fortunately, her saving throw 
succeeds. Marissa\rquote s base eminence score is now a \endash 4, leaving her no chance to smite the kobolds again even if she was silly enough to try it again.
\par 
