\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Extra Thieving Skills: }{\fs24 At 21st level, a thief gains five new skills. The skills have base scores as shown on Table 48 and the character can immediately apply discretionary points to the skills:
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Bribe: }{\fs24 A thief can bribe an NPC with minor gifts of money or merchandise. Only one bribe can be attempted per target. If the attempt fails, the DM should make a reaction roll for the target to determine how he counters the bribe.

\par \tab The amount of money required for a bribe varies with the NPC\rquote s status, according to the table below:
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx2160\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\adjustright {\b\fs24 NPC Status\tab Bribe
\par }{\fs24 Peasant/Slave\tab 2d4 cp
\par Freeman/Soldier\tab 3d8 cp
\par Merchant/Officer\tab 5d10 cp
\par Noble/General\tab 5d100 cp
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Bribery procedure: }{\fs24 It is best to role play an attempt at bribery, but here are a few guidelines:
\par \tab First, the DM must decide if the NPC in questio
n can be bribed, not everyone is susceptible. No character can be bribed to do something that falls outside his job or station. A simple peasant or a palace guard would not agree to assassinate his king or liege lord for a few coins. However, the peasant 
might offer a party shelter for the night or hide the thief from a search party. A guard might agree to look the other way while the party sneaks out the gate. 
\par \tab Likewise, a simple bribe never induces a character to compromise something he believes in or do
 something that places the character in danger. For instance, an acolyte cannot be bribed to look the other way while the party defiles a holy water font. In any case, an NPC bribed through the use of this skill does not perform any action that takes more
 than a few minutes of effort or places himself in danger\emdash the task must strike the character as something that is quick and harmless.
\par \tab Once the DM decides that the NPC can be bribed, use Table 59 (}{\b\fs24 Encounter Reactions}{\fs24 , from the}{\i\fs24  Dungeon Master Guide}{\fs24 ) to determine how the NPC responds when meeting the thief. Secretly roll 1d20 and find the NPC
\rquote s reaction on the table. Do not tell the player what the reaction is, simply role play the NPC\rquote s response to whatever the thief does.
\par \tab If the die roll results in a friendly reaction, the NPC probably does what the thief wants without a bribe. If not, the thief has to offer something\emdash money, services, a magical item, information, or anything else valuable\emdash 
to sweeten the NPC\rquote s reaction. To determine the exact amount required, roll on the bribery table, above, and multiply the result by the difference between the reaction roll and the highest number on Table 59 that is a friendly result.
\par \tab The player should never be told exactly the NPC\rquote s station, the base bribe value, or the initial reaction roll. Good role\_playing on the DM\rquote s part should allow a perceptive player to guess the first and the last.
\par \tab For example, Jobare, the King of Thieves, approaches a peasant in a friendly manner and starts fishing for information about the local baron. The DM rolls a 13 for the peasant\rquote 
s reaction and checks the number on the first column in Table 59 (because Jobare is acting friendly); the peasant is cautious. The DM decides that the peasant is suspicious, and thinks the thief is a spy or bandit. \ldblquote 
Boy, stranger, you sure do ask a whole lot of questions,\rdblquote  says the peasant while backing away slowly.
\par \tab The DM secretly rolls 2d4 and discovers that the base value of the bribe is 5 cp. However, the highest number that gives a friendly result on the first column of Table 59 is a 7, the difference is 6 (13\endash 
7), so Jobare has to give at least 30 cp (5x6) to make an effective bribe. Once Jobare pays the required amount, the player can make a bribery roll. If it succeeds, Jobare can ask a few simple questions and get equally simple answers.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Detect illusion: }{\fs24 Thieves, masters of deception themselves, can see through visible illusions within their line of sight, up to 90 feet away. They perceive the illusion as a translucent image, seeing through it as though it 
were a light mist. The more real the illusion, the more solid the image.
\par \tab For example, }{\i\fs24 phantasmal force }{\fs24 would be totally translucent, while a }{\i\fs24 simulacrum }{\fs24 would be mostly solid. }{\i\fs24 Demishadow monsters }{\fs24 would be somewhere in between the two. }{\i\fs24 Invisibility }{\fs24 
and other nonvisible effects cannot be discerned.
\par \tab Detection is not automatic, and the thief must spend a round concentrating on the area of effect to discover its illusionary qualities. Knowing that something is an illusion is not necessarily a fail\_safe defense 
against illusionary magic, such as in the case of a }{\i\fs24 simulacrum}{\fs24 .
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Detect magic: }{\fs24 Thieves can spot magical emanations within their line of sight, up to 60 feet away. They can determine the intensity of the magic\emdash dim, faint, moderate, strong, and overwhelming.
 This ability can be blocked by the same things that prevent }{\i\fs24 detect magic}{\fs24  spells from operating.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Escape Bonds: }{\fs24 There comes a time in every thief\rquote s career when his luck runs out and he is apprehended. The ability to escape bonds such as ropes, leather thon
gs, manacles, chains, and even straight jackets is a feat of contortion and determination. The thief must roll to break free of every device binding him. If he\rquote 
s tied at the wrists and at the ankles, he must make two successful rolls to free himself. This skill takes five rounds to use. A thief might hurry his efforts, but he suffers a \endash 
5% penalty for each round he tries to shave from the required time. Locked items also require the thief to successfully pick the locks. A failure on any attempt means that the thief cannot loosen that bond or pick the lock.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Tunneling: }{\fs24 A thief might need to dig a tunnel to get to a cache of riches. His success at tunneling depends on several factors. The tunneling table below shows the time required to dig a 10\_foot tunnel wi
th adequate tools. Every 10 feet, the thief must make a skill check, failure means that the front of the tunnel collapses. It can be re\_dug at the loose earth rate.
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx2160\tx3600\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\adjustright {\b\fs24 Type of Earth\tab Modifier\tab   Time
\par }{\fs24 Sand/loose earth\tab \endash 10%\tab   5 hours
\par }\pard\plain \s2\keep\nowidctlpar\tx2160\tx3600\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\outlinelevel1\adjustright {Packed earth\tab   \emdash \tab 10 hours
\par }\pard\plain \keep\nowidctlpar\tx2160\tx3600\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\adjustright \fs20 {\fs24 Rock\tab +10%\tab 30 hours
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\adjustright {\fs24 
\par Tables 49, 50, and 51 give racial, Dexterity/Aim, and armor adjustments for the skills.
\par \tab If the character point system from the }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Option: Skills & Powers }{\fs24 book is in play, a thief may already have some or all of these new skills. 
In that case, the thief gains 50 bonus character points at 21st level. The character must use the points to buy thieving skills from Table 27 in }{\i\fs24 Skills & Powers}{\fs24 
. If the thief already has 9 or more of the skills listed on Table 27, the character cannot spend all 50 points on thieving skills and is allowed to spend them on proficiencies and high\_
level rogue skills instead. The ability to acquire additional thieving skills is a special bonus for reaching 21st level; it is not normally possible to gain additional thieving skills after the character is created
