\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Improvised Attack: }{\fs24 Rogues with this skill can use unorthodox and unexpected maneuvers to achieve special results in combat. The more unusual the maneuver, the better its chance for success.
\par \tab A rogue can use this skill only once a day, but the character can learn the skill multiple times. For example, a rogue who learns this skill three times can make three improvised attacks each day. An improvised
 attack can have one of the following effects:
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Blinding: }{\fs24 If the attack succeeds, the opponent must save vs. breath weapon or suffer blocked vision (from debris thrown into the eyes, an object wrapped around the victim\rquote s head, or even a hat or helmet dropp
ed over the eyes). The opponent suffers the full penalties for darkness (\endash 4 to attacks, saving throws, and Armor Class and movement reduced to one\_third; see the }{\i\fs24 Dungeon Master Guide}{\fs24 , Chapter 13). Because the opponent\rquote 
s situation is painful or awkward or both, the blindfighting proficiency does not reduce the penalties. Note that many creatures cannot be blinded in an improvised attack because they have no eyes or visual organs.
\par \tab The blinding effect lasts until the opponent clears his vision, which requires a roll of 9 or less on 1d20. The opponent\rquote s Wisdom/Willpower or Dexterity/Balance score affects the roll\emdash 
the DM decides which ability applies. The opponent can attempt to clear his vision at the beginning of each round, but doing so counts as a half move (see the }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Handbook}{\fs24 , Chapter 9, or }{\i\fs24 Combat & Tactics}{\fs24 
, Chapter 1).
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Disarm: }{\fs24 If the attack is successful, the opponent must save vs. petrification or lose the use of one weapon (because it is dropped or has become stuck in something). Recovering a lost
 weapon takes a half move. If the weapon is stuck, the opponent must make a successful open doors roll to get it free.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Immobilize: }{\fs24 If the attack succeeds, the opponent\rquote s entire body becomes entangled or trapped, and he must save vs. paralyzation or cease 
all meaningful movement. The opponent remains unable to move or attack until he works free, which requires a roll of 9 or less on 1d20. The creature\rquote s Strength/Muscle or Dexterity/Aim score applies to the roll\emdash 
the DM decides which ability applies. The opponent can attempt to get free at the beginning of each round, but doing so counts as a half move.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Kill: }{\fs24 If the attack succeeds, the opponent suffers a critical injury that reduces the creature\rquote s hit points to \endash 10 instantly unless the opponent saves vs. dea
th magic. If the save succeeds, there is no effect, but in some cases, the rogue can force the opponent to save again the following round by winning initiative and grappling the opponent. 
\par \tab For example, Jobare has attempted to kill an enemy warrior by slamming a shutter down on his neck. The warrior\rquote 
s saving throw was successful, but his head is still caught under the shutter. Jobare can attempt a grappling attack to get another chance to strangle the warrior.
\par \tab Opponents with more Hit Dice or who are higher level than the rogue are immune to kill effects. In this instance, the thief inflicts normal damage.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Stun: }{\fs24 If the attack is successful, the opponent must save vs. breath weapon or become stunned for 2d4 rounds. The character\rquote s Constitution/Fitness adjust
ment applies to the saving throw. A stunned character is unable to cast spells, attack, or move at more than half speed. The character suffers a \endash 2 penalty to Armor Class, attack rolls, saving throws, and ability checks while stunned.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Trip: }{\fs24 If the attack 
succeeds, the opponent suffers a misstep or other misfortune and must save vs. petrification or fall down. Dexterity/Balance bonuses apply to the saving throw. Creatures that fall while moving at speeds greater than 12 might suffer minor damage (1d3 or 1d
6 points of damage, at the DM\rquote 
s option). In some cases, a fall might be inherently more dangerous, such as when a character falls when climbing or stumbles onto a sharp object. If a fall could result in instant death, use the rules for killing attacks inste
ad. Getting up after a fall counts as a half move.
\par \tab The improvised attack skill requires some imagination and role\_playing ability from the player. An improvised attack must be announced in a round\rquote 
s player declaration phase. During the resolution phase, the player must describe exactly what unusual attack the rogue is employing\emdash 
a simple called shot never qualifies as an improvised attack. The rogue then makes a normal attack roll. Standard combat modifiers apply, as does the rogue\rquote s backstab bonus for rear attacks that qualify. In most cases, the rogue\rquote 
s missile bonus from Dexterity/Aim also applies, because most improvised attacks rely on precision and fast movement. Improvised attacks that involve a blow to the opponent are also subject to Strength/Muscle bonuses.
\par \tab If the attack fails, the improvised attack has no effect. If the attack succeeds, the opponent suffers no damage but must roll a saving throw or suffer the improvised special effect. The 
cleverness and appropriateness of the attack affects the saving throw as follows:
\par 
\par   +6 if the rogue player made no attempt to role\_play or describe the attack. For example, the player says, \ldblquote I blind the dragon with an improvised attack.\rdblquote 
\par 
\par   +4 if the rogue player makes only a feeble attempt to role\_play or describe the attack. \ldblquote Uh, I find some dirt and throw it in the dragon\rquote s eyes\rdblquote 
\par   +2 to +6 if the attack described is physically unlikely to have the desired effect. Trying to trip a horse with a staff is not likely to be effective.
\par \tab The save is automatic if the method described is completely inadequate. For example, no character can strangle a great wyrm dragon with a 12\_inch leather thong, nor can a character blind a storm giant with a glob of oatmeal unl
ess he can reach the giant\rquote s face.
\par 
\par   +2 if the rogue has used the same trick earlier in the adventure. The bonus is +4 if the rogue has used the trick earlier in the same encounter, +6 if the rogue has used the trick on the same creature during this adven
ture, and +8 if the rogue has used the trick on the same creature this encounter. However, see the note on establishing tricks, below.
\par 
\par   \endash 2 if the opponent is surprised.
\par 
\par   \endash 4 if the attack described is particularly appropriate. For example, causing a fall from a wall by slamming a window down on the creature\rquote 
s fingers, entangling an opponent in a fishing net, blinding a creature by tricking it into looking at a dusty object and then blowing the dust into its face, etc.
\par 
\par   The optional saving throw modifiers from Table 36 or from Table 38 can be applied to the saving throw if the DM wishes.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24 Establishing a Trick: }{\fs24 If a rogue has successfully used the same improvised attack in three separate adventures, the character can purchase the improvised attack skill t
he next time he gains a level and make the trick a permanent part of the character\rquote 
s skills. An established trick can be used once per encounter without the usual penalty for multiple use; situational penalties still apply. Using an established trick does not count toward the character\rquote 
s daily allotment of improvised attacks unless the trick is used more than once in a single encounter, in which case the multiple use penalties also apply. Opponents who have fought the rogue previously and are prepared for a
n established trick gain a +6 saving throw bonus against it.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 An Example:}{\fs24  Jobare is collecting his ill\_gotten gains after a game of chance in a seedy inn. When one of the players demands his money back, Jobare asks the fellow to hold out his hand. When the
 man complies, Jobare grabs his own feathered hat and clamps it over the man\rquote 
s face. The DM and the player agree that this is a blinding attack. The DM decides that a roll for surprise is in order, as the man is dutifully holding out his hand and not expec
ting attack. The man rolls a 2 on the surprise die, indicating surprise. Jobare rolls a normal melee attack, adjusts for his Dexterity/Aim score and the fact that the man is surprised. The attack succeeds, and the DM rules that the man must save. vs. brea
th weapon at \endash 2 or be blinded. His saving throw fails, giving Jobare an opportunity to escape.
\par \tab Later, Jobare has a special hat made that is lined with pleats of loose cloth that allow the hat to unfold into a small bag, and it contains a springy headband that helps hold the hat in place, whether the hat is on Jobare\rquote 
s head or on an opponent\rquote s face. Jobare uses his trick hat several times, and when he gains enough experience to advance a level, he decides establish it as a skill (spending one proficiency slot or three character points).
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24 Requirement: }{\fs24 Rogue 16+ 
\par }{\b\fs24 Relevant Ability: }{\fs24 N/A 
\par }{\b\fs24 Success: }{\fs24 N/A\tab }{\b\fs24 Cost: }{\fs24 2 (6)
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Inner Focus: }{\fs24 Rogues with this skill can marshal their personal energies to provide a temporary bonus to their Dexterity, Intelligence, or Constitution scores; it is otherwise similar to the warrior skill of the same name.
\par \tab By concentrating for one round and making a successful skill check, the rogue can improve one of the three eligible ability scores to the value listed on the table below. The improvemen
t lasts one round per character level. Because the majority of the character\rquote s energy is directed to the improved ability score, the other two ability scores are reduced by two points each. The rogue can end the boost\emdash 
and restore the values of the other two ability scores\emdash at any time. If the character\rquote s ability score is already higher than the value listed on the table, this skill has no effect. If the optional subabilities rule from }{\i\fs24 
Skills & Powers }{\fs24 is in play, both subabilities in the increased ability score are raised to the same value.
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx1800\tx2160\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\adjustright {\b\fs24 Rogue Level\tab Improved Score
\par }{\fs24 11\endash 15\tab \tab 18
\par 16\endash 20\tab \tab 19
\par 21\endash 25\tab \tab 20
\par 26\endash 30\tab \tab 21
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx360\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab The rogue can take no other actions during the round spent concentrating on raising the selected ability score. Each attempt, successful or not, during a single day lowers the base skill score by two.
\par \tab Bonuses from an increased score are applied immediately when the skill succeeds and are immediately lost when the boost\rquote 
s duration ends. Likewise, the effects from reduced ability scores are applied immediately but then restored when the improvement ends.
\par \tab A character can have only one ability score improvement from this skill operating at any given time.
\par }{\b\fs24 Requirement: }{\fs24 Rogue 11+ 
\par }{\b\fs24 Relevant Ability: }{\fs24 Wisdom/Willpower 
\par }{\b\fs24 Success: }{\fs24 5\tab }{\b\fs24 Cost: }{\fs24 1 (3)
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Nondetection: }{\fs24 Rogues with this skill can shield themselves from many types of magical divination, as the 3rd\_level wizard spell of the same name.
\par \tab When subjected to a spell such as }{\i\fs24 ESP, clairaudience, }{\fs24 or }{\i\fs24 detect invisibility}{\fs24 , or to a magical item such as a }{\i\fs24 crystal ball}{\fs24 
, a successful skill check defeats the spell or device. Even if the roll fails, the rogue is still entitled to any applicable saving throws.
\par \tab This skill is also effective against the sense danger skill, the priest\rquote s detect deception skill, and the ability of intelligent or powerful creatures to detect invisible opponents. When subjected to one of thes
e powers, the rogue can attempt an opposed Wisdom/Willpower check to remain undetected.
\par \tab This skill is not effective against the spells }{\i\fs24 know alignment, true seeing, commune}{\fs24 , or }{\i\fs24 contact other plane}{\fs24 , nor is this skill effective against the detection abilitie
s of legendary monsters, wyrm and great wyrm dragons, and deities.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24 Requirement: }{\fs24 Rogue 16+
\par }{\b\fs24 Relevant Ability: }{\fs24 Wisdom/Willpower
\par }{\b\fs24 Success: }{\fs24 4 \tab }{\b\fs24 Cost: }{\fs24 2 (6)
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Sense Danger:}{\b\fs24  }{\fs24 This skill allows rogues to discover threats that are not obvious to less perceptive characters. It is similar to the warrior skill of the same name, but relies on the rogue\rquote 
s intellect rather than on intuition and grants rogues a slightly different set of sensing abilities.
\par \tab The sense danger skill is actually four different subskills, as outlined below. The number of subskills the rogue knows depends on the character\rquote s level: one subskill at 16th\endash 20th level, two subskills at 21st\endash 
25th level, and three subskills at 26th\endash 30th level. If the character purchases this skill twice, the rogue gains one extra subskill. Any improvement to the skill number improves all the subskills the character knows.
\par \tab Each subskill gives the rogue the ability to detect danger in a different form:
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Ambushes: }{\fs24 The rogue can determine if hidden enemies are lurking in any area the character can see well (see Table 62 in the }{\i\fs24 Player\rquote s Handbook}{\fs24 
). The rogue can scan an area roughly 200 yards square in a single round. An area can be scanned only once each turn, and a successful scan reveals approximately how many creatures are hiding in the area and their approximate size.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Concealed Weapons: }{\fs24 The rogue can study a creature to determine if it has any concealed weapons. A successful check reveals any weapons hidden about the creature\rquote 
s person (tucked into clothing, hidden nearby, etc). If faced with an unknown creature, a successful skill check allows the rogue to determine what its attack modes are (claws, teeth, etc.).
\par \tab When the rogue detects a concealed weapon with this skill, the character notes its general size, location, and type\emdash a sap tucked into the back of a character\rquote 
s breeches or a dagger hidden in a sleeve, for example. The character learns nothing else about the weapon. Note that the rogue must be aware of the creature before checking for concealed weapons\emdash 
a rogue cannot attempt to search a statue for concealed weapons because the player suspects it might be a golem. Unlike the warrior\rquote s version of this skill, a rogue can detect magically concealed weapons.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Hostile Intentions: }{\fs24 
A rogue can size up a living creature in plain sight and deduce its level of hostility toward the rogue and his party. A successful roll gives the rogue a general level of hostility: low (a suspicious merchant), medium (a thief from an opp
osing guild), or high (caught by the local wizard with his }{\i\fs24 staff of the magi }{\fs24 
in your backpack). Unlike the warrior ability, the rogue skill does not reveal how well prepared for attack the creature is, just how much it would like to attack. This skill does
 not indicate how likely a creature is to attack, since a creature does not always act on its hostility.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Impending Attacks: }{\fs24 The rogue develops a sixth sense about weapons and other forms of attack aimed at the character. Any time an undetected enemy is pr
eparing an attack, the rogue can attempt a skill check to receive a vague impression about the attacker\rquote s general direction (front, rear, left, right) and distance (close, far). In many cases, the attacker\rquote 
s identity is obvious from the information the ski
ll provides. A successful skill check gives the rogue a +2 bonus on surprise rolls and a +1 bonus to the initiative roll during the first round of combat if the character is not surprised. Unlike the warrior back protection subskill, the rogue must turn t
o face the attacker to negate any applicable rear attack bonuses. If the character is surprised in spite of this skill, the attacker receives all bonuses normally applied to rear attacks, including backstab adjustments.
\par \tab With any subskill, the DM should roll the check secretly. If the roll fails on a roll of 19 or less, the character detects nothing. If the roll fails on a 20, the character gets a false indication\emdash improperly analyzing a creature\rquote 
s level of hostility, noting a concealed weapon where there is none, etc. A false indication has no effect on an impending attack.
\par \tab If the character also has the alertness proficiency, he receives a +1 bonus to his skill score.
\par 
